CT.searchIndex = [{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s1","h":"How a C++ program is built","x":"How a C++ program is built main.cpp Preprocess #include pasted Compile one .o per .cpp Link join .o + libs main Compile error: a problem inside one file. Linker error: declared and called but never defined. Three stages. Know which one an error comes from. C++ is compiled to machine code before it runs: fast, but it trusts you not to make memory mistakes. #include // paste in the declarations for std::cout int main() { // execution starts here std::cout Preprocess : #include literally pastes header files in; #define does text substitution. Compile : each .cpp file becomes an object file on its own. Link : the linker joins object files and libraries into one executable and resolves calls between files. g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra main.cpp -o main ./main Habit to build now Always compile with -Wall -Wextra . The compiler warns about many of the bugs this lesson describes (uninitialized variables, unused values, signed/unsigned comparisons). Interview angle \"Compile error vs linker error?\" A compile error is a syntax or type problem inside one file. A linker error (\"undefined reference\") means a function was declared and called but never defined anywhere you linked."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s2","h":"Variables and types","x":"Variables and types Every variable has a type that fixes how much memory it uses and what you may do with it. Type Holds Typical size bool true / false 1 byte char a character / small integer 1 byte (always, by definition) int whole number 4 bytes long long bigger whole number 8 bytes float / double decimal number 4 / 8 bytes Only char has a fixed size. When size matters use <cstdint> : std::int32_t , std::uint64_t . Initialization: always give a variable a value int a; // BAD: indeterminate value, reading it is undefined behavior int b = 5; // copy initialization int c(5); // direct initialization int d{5}; // brace (uniform) initialization: preferred int e{}; // empty braces: zero-initialized, e == 0 int f{3.7}; // ERROR: braces forbid narrowing double -> int auto g = 5; // g is int: the compiler deduces the type const int h = 9;// cannot be changed after initialization Undefined behavior An uninitialized local holds garbage. Reading it is UB : junk, a crash, or \"works\" until it does not. The signed/unsigned trap unsigned wraps instead of going negative, and a mixed comparison converts the signed side to unsigned. Predict What does this print? std::cout It prints 0 : -1 becomes 4294967295. Beware comparing an int index with vector::size() ."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s3","h":"Operators and control flow","x":"Operators and control flow 7 / 2 // 3 integer division truncates 7 % 2 // 1 remainder 7 / 2.0 // 3.5 one double operand makes the whole thing floating point if (x > 0 && y > 0) { } // && and || short-circuit: right side is skipped if not needed for (int i = 0; i Predict Prefix vs postfix int x = 5; int a = x++; // use x (5), then increment int b = ++x; // increment first, then use std::cout 5 7 7 x++ yields the old value and then increments, so a is 5 and x becomes 6. ++x increments first (x becomes 7) and yields the new value, so b is 7. Classic bug if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and is always true. You meant == . The compiler warns with -Wall ."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s4","h":"Functions and passing arguments","x":"Functions and passing arguments Declare before use; the header holds declarations, the .cpp the definitions. int add(int a, int b); // declaration int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }// definition double add(double a, double b); // overload: same name, different parameter types void greet(std::string who = \"world\"); // default argument Three ways to pass a parameter void byValue(int n) { n++; } // works on a COPY void byRef(int& n) { n++; } // works on the ORIGINAL void byConstRef(const std::string& s) { } // original, read-only, no copy You want to... Use read a small value (int, double, char, pointer) by value: int n read a large object without copying it by const reference: const std::string& s modify the caller's variable by reference: int& n accept \"no object\" as a valid argument by pointer: Widget* w , and test it for nullptr pass a run of elements std::span<int> (C++20) or const std::vector<int>& , not a pointer plus a count get a result back return it by value; big objects such as std::vector are moved, not copied, so no out-parameter is needed Predict What does this print? int a = 1; byValue(a); byRef(a); std::cout 2 . byValue incremented its own copy and threw it away. Only byRef changed a . Step through what a call does to the argument:"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s5","h":"Where variables live: stack, heap, static","x":"Where variables live: stack, heap, static Stack locals, parameters freed at the closing } very fast, about 1 to 8 MB int local = 1; Heap new / malloc lives until you delete it slower, huge int* p = new int(5); Static / global globals, static variables whole program lifetime fixed at startup static int calls; The lifetime rule decides where a variable should live. Region What goes there Lifetime Speed / size Stack local variables, function parameters automatic: freed when the scope { } ends very fast, small (about 1 to 8 MB) Heap (free store) objects created with new / malloc manual: lives until you delete it slower, huge Static / global globals, static variables whole program fixed at startup int counter = 0; // static storage: exists for the whole program void f() { int local = 1; // stack: created on entry, destroyed on exit static int calls = 0; // static storage, but only visible inside f ++calls; int* p = new int(5); // the int lives on the HEAP; p itself is on the stack delete p; } Mental model Stack: a pile of plates, popped automatically. Heap: a storage unit you rent until you hand the key back."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s6","h":"Pointers from scratch","x":"Pointers from scratch A pointer is a variable whose value is an address . Syntax Meaning int* p declare p as \"pointer to int\" &x address-of: the address of x *p dereference: the thing p points to nullptr the pointer that points to nothing Step through it (addresses are made up): Pointers and arrays An array name decays to a pointer to its first element; p + 1 moves one element . int a[3] = {10, 20, 30}; int* p = a; // same as &a[0] std::cout Careful Never dereference nullptr and never read past the end of an array. Both are undefined behavior. C++ will not stop you. What a pointer does not know Only an address and a type: not how many elements, nor who frees them. Root of most pointer bugs. Interview angle \"What is the difference between NULL and nullptr ?\" NULL is a macro for a null pointer constant, usually plain 0 (an integer), so f(NULL) can call f(int) instead of f(char*) . nullptr has its own type and only converts to pointers."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s7","h":"References","x":"References A reference is an alias : another name for an existing variable, not a copy. int x = 1; int& r = x; // r is another name for x r = 5; // x is now 5 Pointer int* Reference int& Can be null yes no (must refer to something real) Must be initialized no (but should be) yes, at declaration Can be re-pointed yes no, bound for life Syntax to use *p , p->member just use the name Use for optional things, arrays, ownership, C APIs function parameters, aliases Predict The reference trap int a = 1, b = 2; int& r = a; r = b; std::cout 22 r = b does not make r refer to b . References cannot be re-bound. It assigns the value of b into the variable r aliases, so a becomes 2. Step through the reference trap:"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s8","h":"const and pointers","x":"const and pointers const int* p data locked p p can move int (read-only) *p = 5 is an error int* const p pointer locked p p cannot move int (changeable) *p = 5 is fine Read right to left. Red = locked, green = free. const before the star protects the data; after the star it protects the pointer. const = \"I will not modify this\". With pointers, either the data or the pointer can be const. Read right to left. int x = 1, y = 2; const int* p1 = // \"p1 is a pointer to const int\" // *p1 = 5; // ERROR: cannot change the int through p1 p1 = // OK: the pointer itself may move int* const p2 = // \"p2 is a const pointer to int\" *p2 = 5; // OK: the int may change // p2 = // ERROR: the pointer may not move const int* const p3 = // neither can change Make everything const that can be."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s9","h":"Dynamic memory: new and delete","x":"Dynamic memory: new and delete new allocates on the heap and constructs; you must delete it or it leaks . int* one = new int(42); delete one; // single object: new / delete int* arr = new int[100]; delete[] arr; // array: new[] / delete[] (must match!) Bug What happened Memory leak you lost the pointer (or forgot delete ) so the memory is never freed Dangling pointer you used a pointer after the memory was freed (see the step-through above) Double delete you freed the same memory twice: undefined behavior, often a crash Mismatch new[] freed with delete , or the reverse Modern C++ Avoid raw new / delete : use std::vector and std::unique_ptr ( Smart Pointers ). Interview angle \"What does new do beyond malloc ?\" It calls the constructor, throws std::bad_alloc on failure (instead of returning null), and returns a correctly typed pointer. delete calls the destructor and then frees the memory."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s10","h":"Arrays, C-strings and std::string","x":"Arrays, C-strings and std::string int a[5] = {1, 2, 3}; // fixed size, remaining elements are 0, NO bounds checking a[10] = 7; // compiles, undefined behavior at runtime void f(int arr[]) { // this is really `int* arr`: the size is lost! sizeof(arr); // size of a POINTER (8), not of the array } char s[] = \"hi\"; // 3 chars: 'h', 'i', and a terminating '\\0' Prefer: Instead of Use Why int a[N] std::array<int, N> knows its size, no decay, same speed new int[n] std::vector<int> grows, frees itself, .at(i) bounds-checks char* strings std::string owns its memory, safe copy, + , .size() , .find() pointer plus a count std::span<int> (C++20) a non-owning view that remembers its size; accepts an array or a vector without copying std::string s = \"hello\"; s += \" world\"; // grows automatically std::cout v = {1, 2, 3}; v.push_back(4); // size is now 4"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s11","h":"The classic memory bugs","x":"The classic memory bugs Most \"mysterious\" crashes are one of these. Bug Example Uninitialized read int n; use(n); Out-of-bounds int a[3]; a[3] = 1; (valid indices are 0 to 2) Null dereference int* p = nullptr; *p = 1; Use after free delete p; *p = 1; Signed overflow INT_MAX + 1 (UB for signed; wraps for unsigned) Returning a reference to a local int& bad() { int local = 42; return local; // BUG: local is destroyed when bad() returns } // the caller gets a dangling reference int good() { int local = 42; return local; // fine: returns a copy of the value } Tooling Test with -fsanitize=address,undefined : it reports leaks, out-of-bounds, use-after-free and overflow with the exact line."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s12","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. Pointer vs reference? A pointer holds an address, can be null, can be re-pointed, and needs * to use. A reference is a permanent alias that must be initialized and cannot be null. Use references for parameters, pointers when \"nothing\" is a valid value or you need to re-point. Stack vs heap? The stack holds locals and is freed automatically when the scope ends; it is fast but small. The heap holds new -allocated objects that live until you free them; it is large but slower and you must manage the lifetime (or let a smart pointer do it). What is a dangling pointer? A pointer to memory that has been freed or has gone out of scope. Dereferencing it is undefined behavior. Setting a pointer to nullptr after delete helps, and smart pointers avoid the problem. const int* vs int* const ? Read right to left. const int* is a pointer to a const int (data is read-only, pointer can move). int* const is a const pointer to an int (pointer is fixed, data can change). delete vs delete[] ? Memory from new must be freed with delete ; memory from new[] must be freed with delete[] so every element's destructor runs. Mixing them is undefined behavior. Deleting nullptr is safe and does nothing. What is undefined behavior? Code the standard places no requirements on: the compiler may assume it never happens, so anything can occur (crash, wrong output, or apparently fine). Examples: out-of-bounds access, null dereference, signed overflow, reading uninitialized memory, data races. How do you pass a large object to a function efficiently? By const reference if the function only reads it, to avoid a copy. By non-const reference if it must modify the caller's object. Keep going Next lesson: Object-Oriented Programming . Your own notes: Module 1: C++ Basics and the learncpp.com recaps (chapters 1 to 13 and 19). Reference: modern-cpp-features and the modern-cpp-tutorial ."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s1","h":"Classes and encapsulation","x":"Classes and encapsulation private: only the class (and friends) protected: plus derived classes public: everyone balance_ (the data) name_ in a base withdraw(), balance() Hide the data, expose operations that keep the invariant true. A class bundles data with the functions that use it. Encapsulation : keep data private, expose only operations that keep it valid. class BankAccount { public: // the interface other code may use explicit BankAccount(double opening) : balance_(opening) {} bool withdraw(double amount) { if (amount balance_) return false; // invariant protected balance_ -= amount; return true; } double balance() const { return balance_; } private: // the implementation, hidden double balance_; }; BankAccount acc{100.0}; acc.withdraw(30); // fine // acc.balance_ = 1e9; // ERROR: private, nobody can break the invariant Specifier Who can access public everyone protected the class itself and classes derived from it private only the class itself (and its friends) \"Valid\" is the class invariant (here: balance never negative). Hide the representation, let constructors build only valid objects, keep the public interface small. No invariant to protect? Use a plain struct . struct vs class Only the default access differs: struct public, class private. Use struct for plain data."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s2","h":"Constructors and destructors","x":"Constructors and destructors A constructor sets an object up; the destructor ( ~Name() ) runs automatically when its lifetime ends. This is the basis of RAII. class Widget { const int id_; std::string name_; public: Widget() : Widget(0) {} // delegating constructor Widget(int id, std::string n = \"none\") : id_(id), name_(std::move(n)) {} // member initializer list ~Widget() { /* release resources here */ } }; Use the initializer list : it initializes members directly. const and reference members can only be set there. Members initialize in declaration order , not list order ( -Wreorder ). explicit on a one-argument constructor blocks silent conversions. Order of construction and destruction Construction: base, members, derived body. Destruction: the exact reverse. Interview angle Construct: base, members, derived body. Destruct: reverse."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s3","h":"Copying and the Rule of 3, 5 and 0","x":"Copying and the Rule of 3, 5 and 0 The compiler generates copy operations that copy every member. For a raw pointer that copies only the address : a shallow copy , and a bug: Fix: a deep copy . Rule of Three : a custom destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment means you need all three. class Buffer { int* data_; public: Buffer(int v) : data_(new int(v)) {} ~Buffer() { delete data_; } // 1. destructor Buffer(const Buffer& o) : data_(new int(*o.data_)) {} // 2. copy constructor (deep) Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& o) { // 3. copy assignment (deep) if (this != &o) { *data_ = *o.data_; } // guard against a = a return *this; } }; Rule What you write When Rule of 0 none of them The goal. Hold resources in members that manage themselves: std::vector , std::string , std::unique_ptr . Rule of 3 destructor, copy ctor, copy assign The class owns a raw resource and must define copying. Rule of 5 the 3 plus move ctor and move assign Same, and you also want cheap moves (see Core Language). class NoCopy { public: NoCopy() = default; NoCopy(const NoCopy&) = delete; // forbid copying outright NoCopy& operator=(const NoCopy&) = delete; }; What the compiler still generates once you declare something You declare Copying Moving nothing generated generated a destructor, a copy constructor or a copy assignment still generated (but deprecated) not generated : an rvalue silently falls back to a copy a move constructor or a move assignment disabled only what you declared A class can quietly lose its moves (a later logging destructor turns every move into a copy). Ask for defaults with = default ; a polymorphic base needs them defaulted too. Never = delete a move : it kills the copy fallback too. For a move-only type, delete the copy operations. Best interview answer \"Rule of Zero: vector and smart pointers make the generated members correct. Rule of Five only when wrapping a raw resource.\""},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s4","h":"Member functions: this , const , static , friend","x":"Member functions: this , const , static , friend class Counter { int n_ = 0; static int instances_; // ONE copy shared by all Counter objects public: Counter() { ++instances_; } ~Counter() { --instances_; } Counter& add(int k) { n_ += k; return *this; } // `this` is a pointer to the current object int get() const { return n_; } // const: promises not to modify the object static int instances() { return instances_; } // no `this`: callable as Counter::instances() friend std::ostream& operator const member function : callable on const objects, cannot modify members (except mutable ). Make getters const. static member function : belongs to the class, no this . friend : access to privates. Use sparingly."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s5","h":"Inheritance","x":"Inheritance Public inheritance models is-a : a Dog works anywhere an Animal is expected. class Animal { public: explicit Animal(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)) {} const std::string& name() const { return name_; } protected: std::string name_; // visible to Dog, hidden from the outside world }; class Dog : public Animal { public: explicit Dog(std::string name) : Animal(std::move(name)) {} // call the base constructor void fetch() const { std::cout Object slicing Animal a = Dog(\"Rex\"); chops off the Dog part and its virtual behaviour. Use pointers or references. final forbids deriving. Prefer composition unless it is truly is-a. Step through slicing:"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s6","h":"Polymorphism and virtual functions","x":"Polymorphism and virtual functions Dog object vptr name_ ... Dog vtable speak() : Dog::speak ~Dog() Dog::speak code A virtual call: follow vptr, index the table, jump. Cost: one pointer per object, one indirection per call. One vtable per class, one vptr per object. p->speak() through a base pointer reaches the derived behaviour only if speak is virtual . Without it the static type decides (compile time); with it the dynamic type (runtime). Try it: How it works: vtable and vptr Each class with virtuals has a vtable ; each object has a hidden vptr to it. Cost: a pointer per object, an indirection per call, usually no inlining. Term Meaning Overloading same name, different parameters, resolved at compile time Overriding a derived class replaces a virtual base function with the same signature, resolved at runtime Hiding a derived function with the same name (but not virtual / different signature) hides the base one struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() = default; // ALWAYS virtual in a polymorphic base }; struct Circle : Shape { double r; explicit Circle(double r) : r(r) {} double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; } // `override`: the compiler checks you really override }; Two famous traps 1. Always write override . If you mistype the signature (forget const ), without it you silently create a new function instead of overriding. 2. Virtual calls inside a constructor or destructor do not reach the derived class. While Base 's constructor runs, the object's dynamic type is Base , so Base::init() is called even if Derived overrides it. Step through a virtual call:"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s7","h":"Abstract classes and interfaces","x":"Abstract classes and interfaces = 0 makes a function pure virtual ; a class with one is abstract (cannot be instantiated). Only pure virtuals and no data: an interface . struct Logger { // interface virtual void log(const std::string& msg) = 0; virtual ~Logger() = default; }; struct ConsoleLogger : Logger { void log(const std::string& msg) override { std::cout > shapes; shapes.push_back(std::make_unique (2.0)); for (const auto& s : shapes) std::cout area()"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s8","h":"Operator overloading","x":"Operator overloading Overload operators only when the meaning is obvious ( + on vectors, == on values, << to print). struct Vec2 { double x{}, y{}; Vec2& operator+=(const Vec2& o) { x += o.x; y += o.y; return *this; } // member: modifies *this bool operator==(const Vec2& o) const { return x == o.x && y == o.y; } }; Vec2 operator+(Vec2 a, const Vec2& b) { return a += b; } // non-member built from += std::ostream& operator Non-mutating binary operators ( + , == ): non-member , so both sides convert. += : member. Keep usual semantics: == with != , < a strict weak ordering. C++20 <=> generates all comparisons. No new operators; :: . .* ?: cannot be overloaded. Callable objects and conversions struct Greater { // a functor: an object you can call like a function bool operator()(int a, int b) const { return a > b; } }; std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), Greater{}); // a lambda is a compiler-made functor class Handle { int id_ = 0; public: explicit operator bool() const { return id_ != 0; } // allowed in conditions only }; Handle h; if (h) { } // OK: contextual conversion // bool ok = h; int n = h; h + 1; // ERROR: explicit blocks accidental conversions operator() makes a class callable (the idea behind lambdas, std::function and comparators). Mark single-argument conversions explicit , as for constructors."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s9","h":"Composition, relationships and design","x":"Composition, relationships and design Animal Dog is-a (inherits) Car Engine owns: dies with Car Team Player uses: lives on its own Doctor Patient association (a pointer) Prefer composition; inheritance is the strongest coupling. \"Is it a, or does it have a?\" Prefer composition unless you need polymorphism. Relationship Meaning C++ shape Lifetime Inheritance (is-a) Dog is an Animal class Dog : public Animal one object Composition (owns) Car has an Engine, which dies with the car member Engine e; part dies with whole Aggregation (uses, shared) Team has Players who exist independently Player* / std::shared_ptr member independent Association Doctor treats Patients reference or pointer parameter independent Liskov Substitution Code using a base must keep working with any derived type. If Square : Rectangle breaks on setting the width, inheritance was the wrong model: it must mean \"behaves like\", not \"shares code\". Related notes in your repo: Module 2: C++ OOP , objects relationships , and the learncpp.com recaps for chapters 14, 15, 21, 23, 24 and 25."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s10","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Why must a base class destructor be virtual? If you delete a derived object through a base pointer and the destructor is not virtual, only the base destructor runs: undefined behavior, and the derived part's resources leak. A virtual destructor makes delete dispatch to the most derived destructor first. Any class meant to be a polymorphic base needs one. What is the difference between overloading and overriding? Overloading: several functions with the same name and different parameters in the same scope, chosen at compile time. Overriding: a derived class provides its own implementation of a base virtual function with the same signature, chosen at runtime. What is a pure virtual function and an abstract class? A pure virtual function is declared = 0 and has no required implementation. A class with at least one cannot be instantiated; it defines an interface that derived classes must implement. What is the Rule of Three / Five / Zero? Three: if you define a destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment, define all three. Five: add the move constructor and move assignment. Zero: design classes so none are needed by holding resources in self-managing members like vector and unique_ptr . Remember that declaring a destructor or a copy operation suppresses the move operations, so moves silently become copies. What is object slicing? Copying a derived object into a base-class value discards the derived members and virtual behaviour. Avoid it by passing polymorphic objects by reference or pointer. What does explicit do? It stops a constructor (or conversion operator) from being used for implicit conversions. Without it, void f(Account); f(5); could silently build an Account from 5. Can a constructor be virtual? Can a destructor be pure virtual? A constructor cannot be virtual: the vptr is set up by the constructor, so there is no dynamic type yet. A destructor can be pure virtual to make a class abstract, but it still needs a body because derived destructors call it. Composition or inheritance? Prefer composition. Use public inheritance only for a true is-a relationship where you need runtime polymorphism, and make sure it satisfies Liskov substitution."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"s1","h":"RAII: the central idea","x":"RAII: the central idea RAII : tie a resource (memory, file, lock, socket) to an object. Acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor. Destructors run even when an exception is thrown, so nothing leaks. class File { std::FILE* f_; public: explicit File(const char* path) : f_(std::fopen(path, \"r\")) {} // acquire ~File() { if (f_) std::fclose(f_); } // release File(const File&) = delete; // one owner only File& operator=(const File&) = delete; }; Watch a throw in the middle: vector , unique_ptr , lock_guard , fstream are all RAII. Never manage a resource with a bare new / delete , lock() / unlock() or fopen / fclose : wrap it. Interview angle RAII binds resource lifetime to object lifetime: automatic, exception-safe cleanup. It replaces try/finally and removes the need for a garbage collector."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"raii-types","h":"RAII everywhere: guards and standard wrappers","x":"RAII everywhere: guards and standard wrappers Almost every resource has a standard RAII wrapper. Use it instead of a manual acquire/release pair. Resource RAII type Released by heap memory vector , string , unique_ptr destructor frees the block a file std::fstream destructor closes it a mutex lock_guard , unique_lock , scoped_lock destructor unlocks a thread std::jthread (C++20) destructor joins anything else your own scope guard destructor runs a cleanup template class ScopeExit { // runs F when the scope ends, however it ends F f_; bool active_ = true; public: explicit ScopeExit(F f) : f_(std::move(f)) {} ScopeExit(const ScopeExit&) = delete; // a guard must not be copied void release() { active_ = false; } // commit: cancel the cleanup ~ScopeExit() { if (active_) f_(); } }; void transfer() { begin(); ScopeExit rollback([] { undo(); }); // runs on return AND on an exception step1(); step2(); // any of these may throw rollback.release(); // success: keep the changes } A RAII class owns one resource. Copying it must be deleted (one owner) or a deep copy ; moving transfers ownership (the Rule of Five, OOP lesson ; Move Semantics ). Prefer the Rule of Zero : hold resources in members that are already RAII, and you write no destructor at all. Destructors must not throw (next sections). Avoid a naked new : before C++17, f(std::unique_ptr<A>(new A), g()) could leak if g() threw between the new and the unique_ptr constructor. C++17 closed that gap, but std::make_unique is still the idiom: no raw new , no repeated type. Interview angle \"What is RAII?\" The resource's lifetime is tied to an object: acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor. Destructors run on every exit path, including exceptions, so nothing leaks and no finally is needed."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"s7","h":"Exceptions and stack unwinding","x":"Exceptions and stack unwinding double divide(double a, double b) { if (b == 0) throw std::invalid_argument(\"division by zero\"); return a / b; } try { divide(1, 0); } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) { // catch by const reference std::cerr Throw by value, catch by const& (catching by value slices a derived exception). Stack unwinding : leaving scopes runs destructors, in reverse order of construction, until a catch matches. This is why RAII is exception-safe. A destructor must never throw : if it throws while another exception is unwinding, std::terminate is called. Destructors are implicitly noexcept . A constructor that throws leaves no object: the destructors of already-built members and bases run, its own does not. catch (...) catches everything; rethrow with a bare throw; (never throw e; , which copies and slices). Derive your own exceptions from std::exception (or a standard subclass) so generic handlers still work. Cost The no-throw path is almost free, but a throw is orders of magnitude slower than a return and its latency is unpredictable. Do not throw on a hot path or as ordinary control flow ( next sections )."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"guarantees","h":"Exception-safety guarantees and noexcept","x":"Exception-safety guarantees and noexcept Level Promise if an exception escapes Typical example No-throw it never throws destructors, swap , moves, anything noexcept Strong state is exactly as before (commit or rollback) vector::push_back when the element's move is noexcept Basic nothing leaks and invariants hold, but values may have changed most operations Getting the strong guarantee: build aside, then swap void Table::replaceAll(const std::vector & fresh) { std::vector copy(fresh); // may throw: *this is still untouched rows_.swap(copy); // commit: swap cannot throw } Do everything that can fail on a copy , then publish the result with an operation that cannot throw. If anything throws before the swap, the object is unchanged. noexcept noexcept promises a function will not throw. If it does anyway, the program calls std::terminate : there is no unwinding past it. Destructors are noexcept by default. A throwing destructor during unwinding means two exceptions at once, and the program terminates. Mark move operations and swap noexcept when they cannot throw. std::vector grows by moving its elements only if the move constructor is noexcept ; otherwise it copies , to keep the strong guarantee. Compiled example: growing a vector whose elements have a noexcept move made 0 copies; with a possibly-throwing move it copied every element. noexcept(expr) asks the compiler whether an expression can throw; generic code uses it to pick the cheap path ( std::move_if_noexcept ). Do not mark a function noexcept just to be fast: a wrong promise turns an error into a crash."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"errors","h":"Exceptions, error codes and optional","x":"Exceptions, error codes and optional Situation Use a failure the caller cannot sensibly handle here (out of memory, broken invariant, constructor failure) exception : it unwinds to code that can cope an expected failure in normal flow (bad input, \"not found\", parse error) return a value that says so: std::optional , an error code or enum, std::expected (C++23) a hot path with steady latency (feed handler, real time) no exceptions : error codes or optional ( Low-Latency C++ ) a noexcept context, or a destructor never throw: catch inside and log or ignore std::optional parseInt(std::string_view s) { // failure is expected: no exception int v = 0; auto r = std::from_chars(s.data(), s.data() + s.size(), v); if (r.ec != std::errc{} || r.ptr != s.data() + s.size()) return std::nullopt; return v; } if (auto n = parseInt(input)) use(*n); // the caller must look at the result else report(\"not a number\"); Exceptions keep the normal path clean and cannot be silently ignored; the cost is that a throw is slow and the control flow is invisible. Return values make failure explicit and cheap; the cost is that every caller must check, and [[nodiscard]] helps remind them. Constructors cannot return a code: either throw, or use a factory function returning optional or an expected . Be consistent inside a module, and document what a function can throw. Interview angle \"Exceptions or error codes?\" Exceptions for rare failures that need unwinding, return values for expected failures and for hot paths. Mention RAII (so nothing leaks either way) and that a destructor must never throw."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"s11","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. What is RAII and why is it important? It binds a resource's lifetime to an object: acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor. Cleanup is automatic and exception-safe because destructors run on every exit path. It replaces try/finally and is why C++ needs no garbage collector. What is stack unwinding? When an exception propagates, the runtime exits scopes up the call stack, calling the destructors of all fully constructed locals until a handler is found. It is what makes RAII exception-safe. Why should destructors not throw? Destructors run during unwinding. If one throws while another exception is active, std::terminate is called. Destructors are noexcept by default; catch and handle errors inside them. What are the exception-safety guarantees? No-throw (never throws: destructors, swap, moves), strong (if it throws, the state is exactly as before: commit or rollback), and basic (nothing leaks and invariants hold, but values may have changed). Get the strong guarantee by doing the work on a copy and publishing with a non-throwing swap. Why mark move operations noexcept ? std::vector moves its elements when it grows only if the move cannot throw; otherwise it copies them to keep the strong guarantee. A missing noexcept silently turns every reallocation into a round of copies. What happens if a noexcept function throws? std::terminate is called: the exception does not propagate. So only promise noexcept when it is true. Exceptions or error codes? Exceptions for rare failures the local code cannot handle (and constructors); return values ( optional , error codes, expected ) for expected failures and for hot paths where a throw's cost and unpredictability are unacceptable. How do you run cleanup on every path without finally ? Put it in a destructor: a standard RAII wrapper ( lock_guard , unique_ptr , fstream ) or a small scope guard class. Release the guard on success to commit."},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"values","h":"Lvalues, rvalues and value categories","x":"Lvalues, rvalues and value categories An lvalue has a name and an address ( x , v[0] ). An rvalue is an unnamed temporary ( 5 , a + b , a returned value): about to vanish, so its contents can be stolen safely. Category Roughly Examples lvalue a named object x , v[0] prvalue an unnamed temporary 5 , a + b , a call that returns by value xvalue an object marked as movable std::move(x) \"Rvalue\" means prvalue or xvalue. Which reference a value can bind to: Reference type Binds to Typical use T& lvalues only modify the caller's object const T& lvalues and rvalues read-only, avoid a copy T&& (rvalue reference) rvalues only move constructors and move assignment void f(const std::string& s); // accepts anything, copies nothing void g(std::string&& s); // accepts only temporaries: \"I may take your contents\" std::string name = \"abc\"; f(name); // OK // g(name); // ERROR: name is an lvalue g(std::string(\"x\")); // OK: temporary g(std::move(name)); // OK: std::move says \"treat name as a temporary\" int x = 0; static_assert(std::is_same_v ); // double parentheses report the category: static_assert(std::is_same_v ); // lvalue int&, xvalue int&&, prvalue plain int static_assert(std::is_same_v ); A name is always an lvalue Inside g , s is an lvalue despite its && type. Pass it on with std::move(s) (or std::forward , see Templates )."},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"copy-move","h":"Copy versus move","x":"Copy versus move Copy: b = a a 1,000,000 ints b a second copy allocates and copies a million elements Move: b = std::move(a) a: null b same buffer b stole the pointer: two assignments, no copying. The moved-from object is valid but unspecified: destroy it or assign to it. std::move only casts; the move constructor does the stealing. Copying a million-element vector copies a million elements. If the source is a dying temporary, that is wasted work. A move just steals the internal pointer . std::move(x) moves nothing : it is a cast to an rvalue reference meaning \"you may steal\". The move constructor or assignment does the stealing. Overload resolution only looks at the kind of expression: an lvalue picks the copy, an rvalue picks the move. A moved-from object is valid but unspecified : destroy it or assign a new value, do not read it. Compiled: a moved-from std::string had size 0, and assigning to it worked. Interview angle \"What does std::move do?\" Nothing at runtime: an unconditional cast to an rvalue reference. The move constructor does the work."},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"writing","h":"Writing move operations","x":"Writing move operations A class that owns a raw resource needs the Rule of Five ( OOP lesson ). The move operations steal and leave the source safely empty: class Buffer { int* data_; std::size_t n_; public: Buffer(Buffer&& o) noexcept // steal and reset, in one line each : data_(std::exchange(o.data_, nullptr)), n_(std::exchange(o.n_, 0)) {} Buffer& operator=(Buffer o) noexcept { // copy-and-swap: by value covers copy AND move std::swap(data_, o.data_); std::swap(n_, o.n_); return *this; } /* ... constructor, destructor, copy constructor ... */ }; std::exchange(x, v) returns the old x and sets it to v : the idiom for \"steal and reset\". Mark moves noexcept . std::vector moves its elements on reallocation only if the move cannot throw; otherwise it copies them ( exception safety ). The by-value operator= handles self-assignment and gives the strong guarantee. The classic pair operator=(const T&) and operator=(T&&) is faster when you can reuse the existing storage. If you can avoid owning a raw resource, do (Rule of Zero): vector and unique_ptr members make the compiler-generated moves correct."},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"returning","h":"Returning objects: elision and std::move","x":"Returning objects: elision and std::move Compiled with a counting type (copies / moves performed): What you write Copies Moves Why return Counter(); 0 0 guaranteed elision (C++17): built in the caller's storage Counter c; return c; 0 0 NRVO: allowed, not required, but done in practice return std::move(c); 0 1 blocks NRVO: an extra move, so never write it return a by-value parameter 0 1 no elision for parameters, but it is moved, not copied return member; 1 0 a member is never moved implicitly return std::move(member); 0 1 here std::move is right: the object keeps living, you take its member Buffer make() { Buffer b(1000); return b; } // fine: elided or moved automatically Buffer take(Source& s) { return std::move(s.buffer); } // member of an object that outlives the call: move it out Rule Return locals and temporaries plainly. Use std::move on a member , or on something that is not the local you are returning by name."},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"traps","h":"Sink parameters and move traps","x":"Sink parameters and move traps Taking a parameter you will store class Person { std::string name_; public: explicit Person(std::string n) : name_(std::move(n)) {} // by value, then move into the member }; Compiled with a counting type: Holder(lvalue) and Holder(rvalue) together made 1 copy and 2 moves : the lvalue is copied once into the parameter, and each call moves once into the member. For a constructor that stores its argument , take it by value and move. Not for setters: the extra construction can cost more than assigning. Traps Trap What happens std::move on a const object copies : a const T&& cannot bind to T&& , so the copy constructor is chosen (compiled: 1 copy, 0 moves) a type with no move operations silently copies using an object after std::move valid but unspecified; only destroy or assign to it std::move on a forwarding reference parameter moves an lvalue the caller still wanted: use std::forward moving inside a loop the second iteration sees a moved-from object push_back(lvalue) copies, push_back(temporary) moves, emplace_back(args...) builds in place (compiled together: 1 copy, 1 move). Move only the last use After auto b = std::move(a); treat a as empty. Never return const values by value: it disables the move."},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"cheat","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. What does std::move actually do? Nothing at runtime: it is an unconditional cast to an rvalue reference. It enables the move constructor or move assignment to be chosen. The moved-from object stays valid but unspecified. On a const object it silently copies, because a const rvalue cannot bind to a move constructor. What are lvalues, prvalues and xvalues? An lvalue is a named object with an address. A prvalue is an unnamed temporary (a literal, a + b , a call returning by value). An xvalue is an object marked as movable, such as std::move(x) . Rvalue means prvalue or xvalue. A named rvalue-reference parameter is still an lvalue inside the function. What is copy elision / RVO? The compiler constructs a returned object directly in the caller's storage, skipping the copy or move. In C++17 it is guaranteed when returning a temporary (prvalue). NRVO for a named local is allowed but not required. Do not write return std::move(local) : it prevents it and costs a move. Why mark a move constructor noexcept ? std::vector moves its elements on reallocation only if the move cannot throw; otherwise it copies them to keep the strong guarantee. Without noexcept every growth becomes a round of copies. What state is a moved-from object in? Valid but unspecified: you may destroy it or assign a new value to it, but not rely on its contents. For standard types it is usually empty. How do you write a class that stores a string argument efficiently? Take the parameter by value and std::move it into the member. An lvalue costs one copy plus one move, an rvalue costs one move, and there is a single function to write. What is the difference between push_back and emplace_back ? push_back takes an existing object (copied or moved into the container). emplace_back forwards its arguments and constructs the element in place, saving a temporary. Both reallocate the same way."},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"ownership","h":"Who owns it? The three smart pointers","x":"Who owns it? The three smart pointers RAII wrappers around new / delete ( <memory> ) that make ownership explicit in the type . The owner is whoever must free the object. Type Ownership Copyable? Cost std::unique_ptr<T> exclusive: one owner no , only movable same as a raw pointer std::shared_ptr<T> shared: reference counted, freed when the last owner goes yes (bumps the count) extra control block, atomic count std::weak_ptr<T> none: observes a shared_ptr without keeping it alive yes small raw pointer / reference none: \"I use it, I do not own it\" yes none Default A plain value member or local first. Then unique_ptr when it must live on the heap (polymorphism, large, optional). shared_ptr only for truly shared ownership. A function that... takes only uses the object const Widget& (or Widget* if it may be null): never the smart pointer takes over ownership std::unique_ptr<Widget> by value: the caller must std::move keeps a share std::shared_ptr<Widget> by value gives ownership to the caller returns std::unique_ptr<Widget> (a factory) std::unique_ptr makeWidget(std::string n); // factory: sole ownership to the caller void use(const Widget& w); // just uses: pass the object, not the owner void take(std::unique_ptr w); // takes ownership auto w = makeWidget(\"alpha\"); use(*w); take(std::move(w)); // w is now null"},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"unique","h":"unique_ptr in detail","x":"unique_ptr in detail auto p = std::make_unique (1, \"a\"); // preferred over `new` // auto q = p; // ERROR: unique_ptr cannot be copied auto q = std::move(p); // OK: ownership moves, p is now null Zero overhead: with the default deleter sizeof(unique_ptr<T>) == sizeof(T*) (checked). A function-pointer deleter makes it twice as big; a stateless lambda deleter adds nothing. Custom deleter for anything that is not delete : std::unique_ptr<std::FILE, decltype(closer)> f(std::fopen(...), closer); closes the file on scope exit. Arrays: std::make_unique<int[]>(3) uses delete[] . Prefer std::vector unless you need a fixed block. Polymorphism: unique_ptr<Base> holding a Derived needs a virtual destructor on Base , as with any delete through a base pointer. .get() returns the raw pointer without giving up ownership; .release() gives it up (you must delete it); .reset() destroys the object now. Interview angle Why make_unique ? No naked new , the type is written once, and before C++17 f(unique_ptr<A>(new A), g()) could leak when g() threw between the two steps."},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"shared","h":"shared_ptr and the control block","x":"shared_ptr and the control block s1 shared s2 shared w weak control block strong count: 2 weak count: 1 Widget Object freed when strong count hits 0. weak_ptr.lock() gives a shared_ptr or null. unique_ptr has no control block: zero overhead, one owner. auto s1 = std::make_shared (2, \"b\"); // one allocation for object + control block auto s2 = s1; // two owners, use_count() == 2 A shared_ptr is two pointers : to the object and to a control block holding the strong count, the weak count and the deleter. make_shared allocates object and control block together (one allocation). shared_ptr<T>(new T) allocates twice. The deleter is not part of the shared_ptr type (unlike unique_ptr ): type-erased in the control block. The counts are atomic : copying or destroying shared_ptr s on several threads is safe. The object is not protected, and changing the same shared_ptr variable from two threads is a race. Copying costs an atomic increment. On hot paths pass const shared_ptr& or the object itself, not a copy per call."},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"weak","h":"Reference cycles and weak_ptr","x":"Reference cycles and weak_ptr If A holds a shared_ptr<B> and B holds a shared_ptr<A> , each keeps the other alive: neither is ever destroyed . Compiled: after the scope, both objects were still alive (leaked). Make the back link (child to parent) a weak_ptr : both were destroyed. struct Node { std::shared_ptr parent; }; // cycle: leak struct WNode { std::weak_ptr parent; }; // weak back-pointer: freed std::weak_ptr w = s1; // observe only: does not change use_count if (auto sp = w.lock()) { /* still alive: use sp for as long as you hold it */ } // lock() gives a shared_ptr or null w.expired(); // true once every owner is gone lock() is the only way to use the object: it gives a shared_ptr that keeps it alive while you work, or null. Typical uses: parent links, caches, observer lists that must not keep subscribers alive. A weak_ptr keeps the control block alive (not the object) until the last weak reference goes."},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"pitfalls","h":"Pitfalls and enable_shared_from_this","x":"Pitfalls and enable_shared_from_this One control block per object auto raw = new Widget; std::shared_ptr a(raw); std::shared_ptr b(raw); // a second, independent control block: double delete! Never build a second shared_ptr from a raw pointer or from this . Copy an existing one. When a member function needs a shared_ptr to its own object (to register itself, to hand to a callback), inherit std::enable_shared_from_this and call shared_from_this() : struct Session : std::enable_shared_from_this { std::shared_ptr self() { return shared_from_this(); } // a safe second owner, SAME control block }; auto s = std::make_shared (); auto t = s->self(); // use_count() == 2 Session raw; raw.self(); // throws std::bad_weak_ptr: no shared_ptr owns it (compiled) Pitfall Fix shared_ptr cycle weak_ptr for the back link shared_ptr from this or a raw pointer enable_shared_from_this shared_ptr by default \"to be safe\" unique_ptr : you can always convert to shared later storing .get() somewhere that outlives the owner keep the smart pointer, or a weak_ptr delete on a .get() result never: the smart pointer will delete it again"},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"cheat","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. unique_ptr vs shared_ptr vs weak_ptr ? unique_ptr : sole owner, move-only, zero overhead. shared_ptr : reference-counted shared ownership with a control block and atomic count. weak_ptr : non-owning observer used to break cycles and to check whether a shared object is still alive ( lock() ). Is shared_ptr thread-safe? The reference count is atomic, so copying and destroying shared_ptr s from different threads is safe. The pointed-to object is not protected: concurrent access to it still needs synchronization. Concurrently modifying the same shared_ptr variable is also a race. Why prefer make_unique / make_shared ? No naked new , less typing (and exception safe before C++17), and make_shared allocates the object and its control block once instead of twice. How do you break a shared_ptr cycle? Make one direction (usually the child's link to its parent) a weak_ptr . It does not count toward ownership, so the objects can be destroyed; use lock() to get a temporary shared_ptr when needed. How do you pass a smart pointer to a function? If the function only uses the object, pass a reference or raw pointer, not the smart pointer. To transfer ownership, take a unique_ptr by value and have the caller std::move . To share, take a shared_ptr by value. Return unique_ptr from factories. Why can two shared_ptr s built from the same raw pointer crash? Each creates its own control block and count, so the object is deleted twice. Copy an existing shared_ptr , or inherit enable_shared_from_this when an object must hand out a shared pointer to itself. Does unique_ptr cost anything compared with a raw pointer? Not with the default deleter: it is the same size and the destructor inlines to a delete . A stateful deleter (a capturing lambda, a function pointer) makes it larger."},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"anatomy","h":"What a lambda is","x":"What a lambda is [factor](int x) { return x * factor; } captures in [ ] parameters in ( ) body in { } compiles to class __lambda { int factor; // by-value capture int operator()(int x) const { return x * factor; } Capture by reference stores a reference instead; it dangles if the lambda outlives the variable. A lambda is an unnamed function object written in place: the compiler generates a small class whose constructor stores the captures and whose operator() holds the body. // [captures] (parameters) -> return_type { body } auto add = [](int a, int b) { return a + b; }; add(2, 3); // 5 std::vector v{5, 2, 9, 1}; std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int a, int b) { return a > b; }); // descending: an inline comparator Every lambda has its own unique type , so store it with auto (or a template parameter). A lambda with no captures converts to a plain function pointer. Compiled: sizeof of such a lambda is 1, of one capturing an int is 4, of a function pointer 8. operator() is const by default; mutable lifts that (next sections)."},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"captures","h":"Captures: by value and by reference","x":"Captures: by value and by reference int factor = 3; auto byValue = [factor](int x) { return x * factor; }; // copies factor now auto byRef = [&factor](int x) { return x * factor; }; // refers to the live variable auto all = [=] (int x) { return x * factor; }; // [=] all by value, [&] all by reference Predict Capture by value vs by reference int x = 1; auto byVal = [x] { return x; }; auto byRef = [&x] { return x; }; x = 2; std::cout 12 byVal took a snapshot of x (1) when it was created. byRef sees the variable itself, so it reads the new value 2. A by-value capture is const inside the lambda unless you add mutable . Step through the two capture modes: Dangling captures A lambda that captures a local by reference and then outlives it (returned from the function, or run later on another thread) reads destroyed memory. When a lambda escapes its scope, capture by value. [=] and this Inside a member function [=] also captures this (the pointer), so the lambda uses the object through that pointer and dangles if the object dies. This implicit capture is deprecated in C++20: write [=, this] to say so, or [*this] to copy the object."},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"advanced","h":"mutable , init-capture, generic and recursive lambdas","x":"mutable , init-capture, generic and recursive lambdas auto count = [n = 0]() mutable { return ++n; }; // own state; mutable lets it change count(); count(); count(); // 1 2 3 (compiled: \"123\") auto owned = std::make_unique (42); auto init = [p = std::move(owned)] { return *p; }; // C++14 init-capture: MOVE a move-only object in // (compiled: returns 42, the original is null) auto bigger = [](const auto& a, const auto& b) { return a Init-capture [name = expr] creates a new member: the way to move a unique_ptr in, or to precompute something. mutable changes the lambda's own copy; each copy of the lambda has its own state. A generic lambda has a templated operator() : auto parameters are template parameters. A lambda cannot name itself. For recursion, pass it to itself: auto fact = [](auto self, int n) -> int { return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * self(self, n - 1); }; (compiled: 5! = 120), or use std::function . [this] in a member function captures the pointer; [*this] copies the object."},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"callables","h":"Function pointer, functor, lambda, std::function","x":"Function pointer, functor, lambda, std::function Four ways to pass \"something callable\": Kind Can hold state? Inlinable? Overhead function pointer no rarely (indirect call) 8 bytes functor (class with operator() ) yes yes: type known at compile time size of its members lambda yes (captures) yes: it is a functor size of its captures std::function<R(Args...)> yes no: type-erased indirect call, and may allocate template void apply(F f) { f(1); } // template parameter: the lambda's exact type, fully inlined void apply2(std::function f) { f(1); } // type-erased: accepts anything, one indirect call std::vector > ops{ [](int x) { return x + 1; }, [k = 10](int x) { return x * k; } }; // different lambdas, one type Templates for hot paths and algorithms ( std::sort takes its comparator as a template parameter and inlines it). std::function when you must store callables of different types or keep them behind an interface. std::function may allocate when the callable does not fit its small buffer. Compiled with libstdc++: a small capture did not allocate, a 64-byte capture did. std::invoke(f, args...) calls anything callable, including a member function pointer: std::invoke(&Account::balance, acct) . A stateless lambda converts to a function pointer, handy for C APIs and callbacks. Interview angle \"Lambda vs std::function ?\" A lambda has its own type and is inlined; std::function erases the type, costs an indirect call and maybe an allocation. Use it only when you must store different callables in one place."},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"cheat","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. What is a lambda and what does the compiler generate? An unnamed function object: the compiler creates a unique class with the captured variables as members and the body as operator() . Because it has its own type, calls can be inlined. Capture by value vs by reference? By value copies the variable when the lambda is created (const inside unless mutable ). By reference stores a reference to the live variable and dangles if the lambda outlives it. Capture by value for lambdas that escape the scope or run on another thread. How do you move a unique_ptr into a lambda? With a C++14 init-capture: [p = std::move(ptr)] { ... } . A plain capture would need a copy, which unique_ptr forbids. What does mutable do on a lambda? It makes operator() non-const so the lambda can modify its by-value captures. The changes live in the lambda's own copy, not in the original variable. std::function vs a template parameter? A template parameter keeps the exact callable type, so the call is inlined and nothing allocates. std::function erases the type: one indirect call, possible allocation, but it can store different callables behind one type. What is a generic lambda? A lambda with auto parameters (C++14): its operator() is a template, so one lambda works for any argument type. What is wrong with capturing this by [=] ? [=] inside a member function captures the this pointer, not a copy of the object, so the lambda dangles if the object is destroyed first. It is deprecated in C++20: write [=, this] or [*this] to copy."},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"init","h":"Initialization and brace traps","x":"Initialization and brace traps int a; // default-initialized: INDETERMINATE value, never read it int b{}; // value-initialized: zero int c = 5; // copy-initialized int d(5); // direct-initialized int e{5}; // list-initialized: the preferred form, rejects narrowing // int f{3.7}; // ERROR: narrowing double -> int inside braces int g = 3.7; // compiles: silently truncates to 3 Kind Example Notes aggregate Point p{1, 2}; no constructors: members set in order designated (C++20) Point p{.x = 3, .y = 4}; names the members, order must match value-initialize Point p{}; every member zero Braces prefer initializer_list std::vector v1(3, 7); // parentheses: 3 elements, each 7 std::vector v2{3, 7}; // braces: the initializer_list constructor wins -> the two elements 3 and 7 If a class has an initializer_list constructor, {...} picks it even when another constructor matches better (compiled: Widget w{1, 2} called the list constructor, Widget w(1, 2) the (int, int) one). Empty braces {} call the default constructor. Initialize everything. An uninitialized local is indeterminate ; reading it is undefined behaviour. Braces catch accidental narrowing at compile time: a free safety check. A static_cast in braces ( int{x} ) is a conversion that must not narrow."},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"deduction","h":"auto , decltype and modern syntax","x":"auto , decltype and modern syntax int x = 0; const int& r = x; auto a = r; // int auto drops const and the reference decltype(r) b = x; // const int& decltype gives the exact declared type decltype(auto) c = r; // const int& decltype(auto) = \"deduce like auto, but keep it exact\" auto t = {1, 2, 3}; // std::initializer_list , NOT a vector auto traps auto y = v[0]; copies : write const auto& to avoid it. Same for for (auto [k, v] : map) (a copy of each pair). auto hides what a function returns: fine for iterators and lambdas, poor for public interfaces. Feature Instead of Example Range-based for index / iterator loops for (const auto& x : v) {} nullptr NULL / 0 int* p = nullptr; using alias typedef using Map = std::map<int, std::string>; override / final hoping you matched the signature void f() override; = default / = delete empty bodies, private unimplemented copy Foo(const Foo&) = delete; static_assert runtime checks of compile-time facts static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4); Structured bindings (C++17) .first / .second for (auto& [key, val] : m) {} if with initializer (C++17) variable leaking into outer scope if (auto it = m.find(k); it != m.end()) {} [[nodiscard]] ignored error codes [[nodiscard]] bool open();"},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"conversions","h":"Implicit conversions and enum class","x":"Implicit conversions and enum class The compiler converts silently between arithmetic types. Compiled results of the classic surprises: Expression Result Why -1 < 1u false the signed value is converted to unsigned and becomes huge uint8_t(200) + uint8_t(100) 300, stored back as 44 small types are promoted to int before arithmetic; storing back wraps 7 / 2 3 integer division; 7 / 2.0 is 3.5 static_cast<int>(-3.9) -3 conversion to an integer truncates toward zero char 0xFF widened to int -1 (signed char ) whether plain char is signed is implementation-defined Usual arithmetic conversions: smaller than int becomes int ; mixing signed and unsigned of the same rank converts to unsigned; int with double becomes double . Never compare signed with unsigned ( -Wsign-compare ); use std::ssize or cast deliberately. Single-argument constructors are implicit conversions: mark them explicit ( OOP lesson ). enum Color { Red, Green }; // plain enum: leaks names into the scope, converts to int silently int n = Green; // compiles enum class Level { Info = 1, Error = 2 };// scoped: Level::Error, no implicit conversion int m = static_cast (Level::Error); // an explicit cast is required (compiled: 2) Prefer enum class , with an explicit underlying type when the size matters: enum class Side : std::uint8_t { Buy, Sell };"},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"casts","h":"Casts and type conversion","x":"Casts and type conversion Four named casts replace the C cast (T)x : each states intent and is searchable. Cast Use for Danger static_cast<T>(x) well-defined conversions: int to double , base to derived pointer when you are sure low dynamic_cast<T>(x) checked downcast in a polymorphic hierarchy. Returns nullptr (pointer) or throws std::bad_cast (reference) on failure runtime cost; base needs a virtual function const_cast<T>(x) add or remove const (legacy APIs) modifying a truly const object is UB reinterpret_cast<T>(x) reinterpret raw bits (pointer to integer, unrelated pointer types) very high, rarely correct double avg = static_cast (total) / count; // avoids integer division Animal* a = getAnimal(); if (auto* d = dynamic_cast (a)) { // safe downcast: null if it is not a Dog d->fetch(); } Rule Many casts mean a design problem. Prefer virtual functions to dynamic_cast chains; no C casts."},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"const","h":"const , constexpr and static","x":"const , constexpr and static const : unchangeable after initialization. Use everywhere. constexpr : computable at compile time (array sizes, template arguments), and still callable at runtime. constexpr int square(int x) { return x * x; } constexpr int N = square(4); // 16, computed by the compiler std::array a; // size 9: legal because it is a constant expression int runtime = readInput(); int r = square(runtime); // also fine: same function runs at runtime static_assert(N == 16, \"math is broken\"); // checked at compile time static means three things: Where Meaning Local variable in a function one instance that lives for the whole program and keeps its value between calls Global / namespace scope internal linkage: visible only inside this .cpp file Class member belongs to the class, shared by all objects"},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"standards","h":"What C++14, 17 and 20 added","x":"What C++14, 17 and 20 added \"What modern features do you use?\" Know these and why: Standard Feature One-liner C++11 move semantics, smart pointers, lambdas, auto , constexpr , std::thread , variadic templates, nullptr , range-for The big modernisation. C++14 generic lambdas, make_unique , return type deduction, binary literals 0b101 Polishes C++11. C++17 std::optional , std::variant , std::string_view , structured bindings, if constexpr , fold expressions, std::filesystem , parallel algorithms, std::scoped_lock , CTAD Vocabulary types and compile-time branching. C++20 concepts, ranges, std::span , operator<=> , coroutines, modules, std::jthread , std::format Constraints on templates, composable algorithms. std::optional parse(const std::string& s); // \"maybe an int\" without magic values if (auto n = parse(\"42\")) { use(*n); } std::variant v = \"hi\"; // type-safe union std::visit([](auto&& x) { std::cout Full lists: modern-cpp-features , modern-cpp-tutorial . Your notes: Module 3 ."},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"cheat","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. When would you use dynamic_cast ? For a checked downcast from a polymorphic base to a derived type when you cannot express the need through a virtual function. It returns null (pointers) or throws bad_cast (references) on failure. Frequent use suggests a design problem. The four named casts? static_cast for well-defined conversions, dynamic_cast for checked downcasts, const_cast to add or remove const (modifying a truly const object is UB), reinterpret_cast to reinterpret bits (rarely correct). Never a C cast: it can silently pick any of them. Difference between const and constexpr ? const means read-only after initialization (the value may be determined at runtime). constexpr means the value or function can be evaluated at compile time and used in constant expressions. What does auto deduce, and what are its traps? The type of the initializer with top-level const and references dropped. auto x = {1,2,3} is an initializer_list . auto y = v[0] copies; use const auto& . decltype(expr) keeps the exact type. What is wrong with if (-1 < 1u) ? The signed operand is converted to unsigned, becoming a huge value, so the comparison is false. Avoid mixing signed and unsigned; compile with -Wsign-compare . Why prefer enum class ? Its names are scoped ( Level::Error ) so they do not pollute the enclosing scope, and it does not convert implicitly to an integer. You can also fix its underlying type. std::vector<int> v{3, 7} versus v(3, 7) ? Braces prefer the initializer_list constructor: two elements, 3 and 7. Parentheses call the (count, value) constructor: three elements, each 7. What are the three meanings of static ? A local variable that lives for the whole program and keeps its value between calls; internal linkage for a global (visible only in this .cpp ); a class member shared by all objects. Which C++17 features do you use? std::optional , std::variant , std::string_view , structured bindings, if constexpr , if with initializer, fold expressions and [[nodiscard]] : vocabulary types and clearer code."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s1","h":"The big picture","x":"The big picture Containers vector, map, list Iterators [begin, end) Algorithms sort, find, accumulate Algorithms never touch containers, only iterator ranges. One sort works on any container that offers the right iterators. Part What it is Examples Containers classes that own and organise a collection of elements vector , map , set , deque Iterators generalised pointers that walk a container v.begin() , m.find(k) Algorithms generic functions that operate on iterator ranges sort , find , accumulate Algorithms see only [first, last) , so one std::sort serves a vector, array or deque. That separation is the design. #include // std::vector #include // std::map, std::multimap #include // std::unordered_map #include // std::set #include // std::sort, std::find, std::transform ... #include // std::accumulate, std::iota #include // std::string Default choice std::vector . Contiguous memory is so cache-friendly it often beats \"better\" containers."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s2","h":"vector : the workhorse","x":"vector : the workhorse buffer on the heap 10 20 30 spare spare size = 3 (elements in use) capacity = 5 (slots allocated) push_back, buffer full: allocate bigger, move all, free old = amortised O(1), but every iterator and pointer into it is invalidated reserve() grows capacity only; resize() changes size. A vector is one contiguous heap array with a size and a capacity . When full, push_back allocates a bigger buffer, moves everything and frees the old one: occasional O(n), amortised O(1) . std::vector v; v.reserve(100); // capacity 100, size still 0: no reallocation for the next 100 push_backs v.push_back(1); // copy/move an existing object in v.emplace_back(2); // construct the element in place: no temporary v.resize(10); // size becomes 10; new elements are value-initialised (0) v[3]; // no bounds check: out-of-range is undefined behavior v.at(3); // bounds-checked: throws std::out_of_range v.front(); v.back(); v.data(); // data() is the raw int* for C APIs v.size(); v.capacity(); v.empty(); reserve vs resize reserve(n) only allocates; resize(n) creates elements. v[5] = x after only reserve(10) is UB. Braces vs parentheses std::vector a(3, 7); // parentheses: size 3, every element 7 -> {7, 7, 7} std::vector b{3, 7}; // braces: the initializer_list constructor wins -> {3, 7} With braces, a constructor taking std::initializer_list is preferred whenever it can match."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s3","h":"Other sequence containers","x":"Other sequence containers Container Storage Best at Watch out std::array<T,N> fixed size, inline (stack) size known at compile time; zero overhead cannot grow std::vector<T> one contiguous heap block almost everything; fast iteration and indexing insert/erase in the middle is O(n) std::deque<T> chunks of arrays fast push/pop at both ends, still indexable not contiguous std::list<T> doubly linked nodes O(1) insert/erase anywhere if you hold an iterator ; iterators stay valid no indexing, a heap allocation per element, poor cache behaviour std::forward_list<T> singly linked nodes minimal memory list forward iteration only Operation vector deque list index [i] O(1) O(1) not available push_back amortised O(1) O(1) O(1) push_front O(n) O(1) O(1) insert / erase in the middle O(n) O(n) O(1) at a known position find a value O(n) O(n) O(n)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s4","h":"Associative and unordered containers","x":"Associative and unordered containers map / set: balanced tree, sorted, O(log n) 20 10 30 in-order walk = sorted order unordered_map: hash table, average O(1) 0 \"Ana\" 1 2 \"Bob\" \"Eve\" hash(key) picks a bucket; collisions chain (Bob, Eve) Need order or range queries: map. Plain lookup: unordered_map. map / set unordered_map / unordered_set Implementation balanced binary tree (usually red-black) hash table Order of iteration sorted by key unspecified find / insert / erase O(log n) average O(1), worst case O(n) Key requirement operator< (a strict weak ordering) a hash function and operator== Range queries ( lower_bound ) yes no std::map age; age[\"Ana\"] = 30; // operator[] INSERTS a default (0) if the key is missing age.insert({\"Bob\", 25}); // does nothing if \"Bob\" already exists age.insert_or_assign(\"Bob\", 26); // C++17: insert or overwrite if (auto it = age.find(\"Ana\"); it != age.end()) // find never inserts std::cout second; // it->first is the key, it->second the value for (const auto& [name, years] : age) // structured bindings; sorted by name std::cout seen; if (!seen.insert(5).second) { /* second == false means it was already there */ } Predict Does looking up make the map bigger? std::map m; if (m[\"x\"] == 0) { } std::cout 1 operator[] on a missing key inserts it with a default-constructed value. To only check, use m.find(\"x\") , m.count(\"x\") or m.contains(\"x\") (C++20). Container adaptors They wrap another container and restrict the interface: std::stack (LIFO: push/pop/top ), std::queue (FIFO: push/pop/front ) and std::priority_queue (a heap: top() is the largest element by default). std::priority_queue maxHeap; // largest first std::priority_queue , std::greater > minHeap; // smallest first Step through operator[] :"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s5","h":"Which container should I use?","x":"Which container should I use? Pick what you mostly do: Interview angle \"vector vs list?\" Answer with memory : contiguous means cache-friendly, so vector wins even for middle inserts at modest sizes. List wins for stable iterators and cheap splicing."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s6","h":"Iterators","x":"Iterators An iterator acts like a pointer: *it , ++it , it == end . end() is one past the last ; ranges are half-open [begin, end) . std::vector v{1, 2, 3}; for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) std::cout Category Can do Containers Forward ++ , read (multi-pass) forward_list , unordered containers Bidirectional also -- list , map , set Random access also it + n , it[i] , it1 - it2 , < vector , deque , array , string So std::sort needs random access and fails on std::list (use list.sort() ). Iterator invalidation Changing a container can invalidate iterators, pointers and references; using one is UB. Container Operation What is invalidated vector push_back , insert all if it reallocated; otherwise those at or after the insertion point vector erase the erased element and everything after it deque insert/erase in the middle everything; push at either end invalidates iterators but not references list , map , set insert nothing list , map , set erase only the erased element unordered_* insert that triggers a rehash all iterators (references stay valid) Spot the bug Erasing inside a loop std::vector v{1, 2, 3, 4}; for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) if (*it % 2 == 0) v.erase(it); // BUG erase invalidates it , and the loop then does ++it on a dead iterator. The correct idiom uses the iterator erase returns: for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ) { if (*it % 2 == 0) it = v.erase(it); // erase returns the next valid iterator else ++it; } // or, in C++20: std::erase_if(v, [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }); Step through a correct erase loop:"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s7","h":"Algorithms","x":"Algorithms Over a hundred generic functions in <algorithm> / <numeric> . Prefer them to loops: named, tested, often faster. Most take a lambda. Try some on {5, 2, 9, 2, 7, 4} : std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](const P& a, const P& b) { return a.age 6; }); int n = std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }); bool any = std::any_of(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x std::transform(v.begin(), v.end(), v.begin(), [](int x) { return x * x; }); auto pos = std::lower_bound(sorted.begin(), sorted.end(), 5); // binary search: range MUST be sorted Predict The remove surprise std::vector v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 3); std::cout 5 Algorithms cannot change a container's size. std::remove shifts kept elements forward and returns the new logical end. Use the erase-remove idiom : v.erase(std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 3), v.end()); // classic std::erase(v, 3); // C++20: one call Strict weak ordering Use < , never <= : a comparator true for equal elements can crash sort or corrupt a set . sort is not stable; use stable_sort ."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s8","h":"Strings and string_view","x":"Strings and string_view std::string s = \"hello world\"; s.size(); s.empty(); s[0]; s.at(0); s += \"!\"; // append s.substr(0, 5); // \"hello\" auto pos = s.find(\"world\"); // index, or std::string::npos if not found if (pos != std::string::npos) { } std::to_string(42); // \"42\" std::stoi(\"123\"); // 123 (throws std::invalid_argument on bad input) std::string_view sv = s; // non-owning view: no allocation, no copy void log(std::string_view msg); // accepts string, literal and substring views cheaply Small-string optimisation, the rules for string_view and parsing are in Strings, Regex & Time ."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s9","h":"Useful companions and pitfalls","x":"Useful companions and pitfalls std::pair p{1, \"a\"}; p.first; p.second; auto t = std::make_tuple(1, 2.5, \"x\"); std::get (t); // 2.5 auto [id, name] = p; // structured binding (C++17) std::optional maybe = std::nullopt; // \"value or nothing\" std::array a{1, 2, 3}; // fixed-size, knows its size Pitfall Fix Using an iterator/pointer/reference after a push_back reallocated the vector reserve up front, or re-fetch the iterator, or index instead map[key] silently inserts use find / contains to test; at() to read-or-throw v.size() - 1 on an empty vector ( size() is unsigned, so it wraps to a huge number) check empty() first or write i + 1 < v.size() Passing a big container by value pass const std::vector<T>& std::vector<bool> is a packed-bit special case with proxy references use std::vector<char> or std::bitset auto x = v[0] copies the element const auto& x = v[0] std::find on a map is O(n) use the member m.find(key) , O(log n) Notes: Module 3 ."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s10","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet How does std::vector grow? When size == capacity it allocates a larger buffer (geometric growth, typically 1.5x to 2x, implementation-defined), moves or copies the elements, and frees the old buffer. This makes push_back amortised O(1), but it invalidates all iterators, pointers and references. reserve avoids the reallocations. map vs unordered_map ? map : balanced tree, sorted iteration, O(log n), needs operator< , supports range queries. unordered_map : hash table, average O(1) (worst O(n) with many collisions), no order, needs a hash and == . Choose unordered_map for plain lookups and map when you need order. push_back vs emplace_back ? push_back takes an existing object (a copy or move). emplace_back forwards its arguments to the element's constructor and builds it in place, avoiding a temporary. For simple types there is no difference; for objects with expensive construction emplace_back can save a move. What is the erase-remove idiom? std::remove / remove_if only moves the elements to keep to the front and returns the new end; it cannot shrink the container. Pass that to the container's erase to actually delete the tail: v.erase(std::remove_if(...), v.end()) . C++20 has std::erase_if . What is a hash collision and how is it handled? Two different keys hash to the same bucket. The standard library's unordered containers resolve it with chaining (a bucket holds a list of entries), so lookups in a heavily colliding bucket degrade toward O(n). A good hash function and a sensible load factor ( rehash , reserve ) keep it near O(1). Why can't I std::sort a std::list ? sort needs random-access iterators (it jumps around by index). A list only provides bidirectional iterators, so it has its own member function list::sort . Which container for a task queue? For \"top K\" elements? A queue ( std::queue , backed by deque ) for FIFO tasks. A priority_queue (heap) for top K: keep a heap of size K and compare against its root: O(n log K)."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s1","h":"Why templates, and function templates","x":"Why templates, and function templates template<T> maxOf a blueprint: generates no code int maxOf(int, int) double maxOf(double, double) string maxOf(string, string) real functions, made on first use Compile-time polymorphism: no runtime cost, more code per type. Without templates: maxInt , maxDouble , maxString ... A template takes the type as a parameter: template T maxOf(T a, T b) { return a (3, 2.5);// explicit: T = double, the int is converted The template is not compiled itself. Each new type instantiates a real function: no runtime cost, more binary size, and errors appear only when a bad type is used. Pick a type: Interview angle Templates: compile-time (static) polymorphism, fastest, inlinable, more code, types known at compile time. Virtual: runtime , one code path, mixed objects, a vtable indirection."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s2","h":"Class templates","x":"Class templates Same idea for classes: vector<T> , map<K,V> , unique_ptr<T> . template // a type parameter, a NON-TYPE parameter, with a default class Stack { std::array items_; std::size_t size_ = 0; public: bool push(const T& v) { if (size_ == Capacity) return false; items_[size_++] = v; return true; } T pop() { return items_[--size_]; } bool empty() const { return size_ == 0; } }; Stack a; // Stack Stack b; // a different, unrelated type std::pair p{1, 2.5}; // C++17 CTAD: class template argument deduction -> std::pair std::vector v{1, 2, 3}; // std::vector Non-type parameters are compile-time constants (how std::array<int,5> knows its size). Each instantiation is a separate, unrelated class. Members are instantiated only if called. Why templates live in headers The compiler needs the body at instantiation. In a .cpp , others see only a declaration: linker errors. Use headers, or explicit instantiation ( template class Stack<int>; )."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s3","h":"Specialization","x":"Specialization A specialization replaces the generic template for specific arguments. template struct Printer { // primary template static void print(const T& v) { std::cout // full specialization for bool struct Printer { static void print(bool v) { std::cout struct Printer { // PARTIAL specialization: any pointer type static void print(T* p) { std::cout Kind Meaning Allowed for Full specialization all parameters fixed: template<> ... Printer<bool> class and function templates Partial specialization a pattern: Printer<T*> class templates only Functions: prefer overloads No partial specialization for function templates. Overload instead; an exact non-template match wins. Function specializations do not take part in overload resolution."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"deduction","h":"Template argument deduction","x":"Template argument deduction When you call a function template, the compiler works out T from the arguments. The rule depends on the shape of the parameter , and it is the same rule auto uses. Parameter Call Deduced T Rule T x (by value) f(ci) with const int ci int a copy: top-level const and references are dropped T x (by value) f(arr) with int arr[3] int* arrays and functions decay to pointers T& x f(ci) const int the reference keeps the argument's const T& x f(arr) int[3] an array keeps its size (usable to get its length) const T& x f(5) int binds temporaries; const is not part of T T&& x f(i) / f(5) int& / int forwarding reference (next section) Compiled and checked with static_assert : by value, const int deduced int and the array deduced int* ; by reference they deduced const int& and int(&)[3] . Deduction looks only at the arguments , never at the return type, and no conversions happen: maxOf(3, 2.5) fails because T would be both int and double . Default arguments and braced lists do not take part in deduction: f({1, 2}) cannot deduce T . Name T explicitly: f<std::vector<int>>({1, 2}) . Name the type yourself when needed: maxOf<double>(3, 2.5) . Class template argument deduction (C++17) std::pair p{1, 2.5}; // std::pair std::vector v{1, 2, 3}; // std::vector std::array a{1, 2, 3}; // std::array template struct Box { T value; }; template Box(T) -> Box ; // a user-defined deduction guide (needed for aggregates before C++20) Box b{std::string(\"x\")}; // Box The compiler deduces the class's arguments from the constructor call. A deduction guide tells it how when the constructors alone are not enough. All of the above was compiled and checked. When deduction fails The error says \"couldn't deduce template parameter\". Look for conflicting arguments, a braced list, or a parameter that does not mention T at all (e.g. only the return type does)."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s4","h":"Forwarding references and perfect forwarding","x":"Forwarding references and perfect forwarding template<class T> void f(T&& x) f(lvalue) s T = X& X& && collapses to X& f(rvalue) string(\"x\") T = X parameter is X&& Collapsing rule: any lvalue reference involved gives an lvalue reference. std::forward restores the original category. One signature accepts both; std::forward passes the category on. T&& in a function template is a forwarding reference , not an rvalue reference: it binds to anything and remembers lvalue vs rvalue. Explore: Is it really a forwarding reference? Only when T is deduced for that function and the form is exactly T&& or auto&& : Declaration Forwarding reference? template<class T> void f(T&& x) yes: T is deduced for f auto&& x = expr; yes template<class T> void f(std::vector<T>&& v) no: not exactly T&& , so it binds rvalues only template<class T> void f(const T&& x) no: const disqualifies it template<class T> struct S { void f(T&& x); }; no: T is fixed by the class, so nothing is deduced for f void f(Widget&& w) no: a plain rvalue reference Reference collapsing Reference to reference collapses: any lvalue reference makes it an lvalue reference; only && + && stays rvalue. Written Collapses to T& & , T& && , T&& & T& T&& && T&& Perfect forwarding A wrapper's parameter has a name , so it is an lvalue and the callee would always copy. std::forward restores the category: template std::unique_ptr makeUnique(Args&&... args) { // forwarding references return std::unique_ptr (new T(std::forward (args)...)); // lvalues stay lvalues, rvalues stay rvalues } std::string s = \"abc\"; auto a = makeUnique (s); // s is an lvalue -> Widget copies it auto b = makeUnique (std::string(\"x\"));// temporary rvalue -> Widget MOVES it std::move vs std::forward move always casts to rvalue. forward<T> casts to rvalue only if the argument was one. Use forward only on a forwarding reference, once. Step through perfect forwarding:"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s5","h":"Variadic templates and fold expressions","x":"Variadic templates and fold expressions A parameter pack ( typename... Args ) takes any number of arguments; args... expands it. Before C++17: recursion with a base case. template T sum(T v) { return v; } // base case: one argument template T sum(T first, Rest... rest) { return first + sum(rest...); } // peel one off, recurse sum(1, 2, 3); The compiler instantiates a chain of real functions: C++17 fold expressions replace the recursion: template auto sum(Args... args) { return (args + ...); } // unary right fold: a + (b + (c ...)) template void print(Args&&... args) { ((std::cout Behind make_unique , make_shared , emplace_back , tuple , thread , function ."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s6","h":"Type traits and if constexpr","x":"Type traits and if constexpr describe<int> is_integral: compiled and kept is_floating_point: discarded else: discarded not even compiled, so they may use code that is invalid for T A normal if would fail to compile as soon as one branch is invalid for T. A template often needs to ask about its type. <type_traits> answers at compile time ( std::is_integral_v<T> ), and if constexpr (C++17) keeps only the matching branch: template std::string describe(const T& v) { if constexpr (std::is_integral_v ) return \"integer \" + std::to_string(v); else if constexpr (std::is_floating_point_v ) return \"float \" + std::to_string(v); else return \"something else\"; } Traits, SFINAE and enable_if , concepts, constexpr , consteval and template metaprogramming have their own lesson: Type Traits & Compile-time C++ ."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"tools","h":"Alias templates, variable templates and more","x":"Alias templates, variable templates and more Tool Example Use Alias template template <typename T> using Table = std::map<std::string, std::vector<T>>; a shorter name that still takes a parameter; unlike typedef it can be templated Variable template (C++14) template <typename T> constexpr T zero = T{}; a constant per type: zero<int> , zero<double> . The standard's std::is_integral_v<T> is one Template template parameter template <template <typename...> class C, typename T> pass a template ( std::vector ) rather than a type: makeTwo<std::vector>(3, 4) Non-type parameter template <typename T, std::size_t N> a value as parameter; auto accepts any type of value in C++17: template <auto V> Abbreviated template (C++20) void f(auto x) each auto parameter is an implicit template parameter template using Table = std::map >; Table t; // std::map > template constexpr T zero = T{}; // zero is 0, zero is 0.0 template class Container, typename T> Container makeTwo(T a, T b) { Container c; c.push_back(a); c.push_back(b); return c; } auto v = makeTwo (3, 4); // a vector with two elements Unpacking with std::index_sequence To call a function with the elements of a std::tuple you need the indices 0, 1, 2... as a pack. std::make_index_sequence<N> generates it: template auto applyImpl(F f, const Tuple& t, std::index_sequence ) { return f(std::get (t)...); } // expands to get , get , ... template auto applyTuple(F f, const Tuple& t) { return applyImpl(f, t, std::make_index_sequence >{}); } applyTuple([](int x, int y, int z) { return x + y + z; }, std::tuple{1, 2, 3}); // 6 (compiled) C++17 ships this as std::apply . Know the idea: a pack of integers lets you expand an expression once per element."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s8","h":"Pitfalls and good practice","x":"Pitfalls and good practice Pitfall Explanation / fix Huge unreadable error messages The error is reported deep inside an instantiation. Read the first error and the line \"required from here\". Concepts or static_assert on traits give early, clear errors. Template body in a .cpp file Linker errors. Put the definition in the header. typename for dependent types Inside a template, T::iterator could be a type or a value. Write typename T::iterator it; . Members of a dependent base class not found Write this->member or Base<T>::member . Code bloat Every distinct T generates more machine code. Move type-independent logic into a non-template helper. Deduction conflicts: maxOf(3, 2.5) Two different deduced T s fail. Cast, specify maxOf<double> , or use two parameters. maxOf(\"a\", \"b\") with const char* Compares pointer addresses, not text. Use std::string or std::string_view . Notes: Module 4 ; modern-cpp-features ."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s9","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet How are template arguments deduced? From the arguments of the call, by the shape of the parameter. By value ( T x ): const and references are dropped and arrays decay to pointers. By reference ( T& x ): const and the array size are kept. T&& is a forwarding reference. No conversions take part, so maxOf(3, 2.5) fails; name T explicitly. What are alias templates and variable templates? An alias template is a parameterised using ( template<class T> using Vec = std::vector<T>; ), which a typedef cannot be. A variable template is a constant or variable per type ( template<class T> constexpr T zero = T{}; ); std::is_integral_v is one. What happens when you use a function template? The compiler deduces the template arguments from the call, then instantiates a concrete function for that combination of types (once per distinct combination across the program), and compiles it like normal code. The template itself generates no code. What is a forwarding reference? A T&& parameter where T is deduced for that very function template (or auto&& ). It binds to lvalues and rvalues alike: for an lvalue T becomes X& and reference collapsing makes the parameter X& ; for an rvalue T is X and the parameter is X&& . std::forward then preserves that category. What is perfect forwarding, and why do we need it? Passing arguments through a wrapper to another function without changing their value category or constness, so the callee can still choose copy vs move correctly. Without it you would need an overload per combination of lvalue/rvalue/const for every parameter. Explain SFINAE. If substituting deduced types into a function template's signature yields an invalid type or expression, that candidate is removed from the overload set rather than raising an error. std::enable_if exploits it to enable a function only for suitable types. C++17 if constexpr and C++20 concepts are the clearer modern tools. Full vs partial specialization? Full: all template arguments fixed. Partial: a pattern with some parameters still free ( Foo<T*> ), allowed for class templates and variable templates but not for function templates (use overloading). Why must template definitions be in headers? The compiler needs the body to instantiate it in each translation unit that uses a particular type. If only the declaration is visible, no instantiation happens and the linker cannot find the function. Alternative: explicit instantiation in one .cpp . What is a variadic template and how do you process the pack? A template taking zero or more arguments via a parameter pack ( typename... Args ). Expand with args... . Process by recursion with a base case, or in C++17 with a fold expression like (args + ...) . Templates vs inheritance for polymorphism? Templates give static (compile-time) polymorphism: no runtime cost and inlinable, but the type must be known when compiling and each type duplicates code. Inheritance with virtual functions gives dynamic (runtime) polymorphism: one code path and heterogeneous collections, at the cost of an indirection and heap-allocated objects."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s1","h":"What patterns are and are not","x":"What patterns are and are not A pattern is a vocabulary : \"this is an Observer\" conveys structure and intent. Three families: Family Concerned with Examples Creational how objects are created Singleton, Factory, Builder Structural how objects are composed Adapter, Decorator, Proxy, PIMPL Behavioural how objects interact and share responsibility Strategy, Observer, State, Command, Visitor Not goals Each pattern adds indirection: use one only for a real problem, and say when not to use it in interviews. Modern C++ shortens many A lambda or std::function replaces Strategy and Command hierarchies; std::visit replaces Visitor; smart pointers are a Proxy; RAII is built in."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s2","h":"Singleton","x":"Singleton code in file A thread B Logger::instance() static local, built once the one Logger Thread-safe creation since C++11; its methods still need their own locking. Intent: exactly one instance with global access (logger, config). class Logger { public: static Logger& instance() { static Logger inst; // created the first time control passes here return inst; // ...and the same object is returned forever after } Logger(const Logger&) = delete; // forbid copies: there must be only one Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete; void log(const std::string& msg) { std::cout The Meyers Singleton : Thread safe creation: a function-local static is initialized exactly once since C++11 (\"magic statics\"): no mutex or double-checked locking. Calls on the object still need their own locking. Lazy: built on first use, avoiding static initialization order problems. Drawbacks (say them): global state in disguise, hidden dependencies, hard to test with a fake, unspecified destruction order at exit. Alternative: create one object in main and pass it by reference (dependency injection)."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s3","h":"Factory","x":"Factory caller Kind::Circle makeShape() unique_ptr Shape Circle Square The caller never names Circle or Square. New type means editing the factory, unless it keeps a registry. Intent: create objects without the caller naming the concrete class; it gets something usable only through an interface. struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() = default; }; struct Circle : Shape { double r; explicit Circle(double r) : r(r) {} double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; } }; struct Square : Shape { double s; explicit Square(double s) : s(s) {} double area() const override { return s * s; } }; enum class Kind { Circle, Square }; std::unique_ptr makeShape(Kind k, double size) { // the factory function switch (k) { case Kind::Circle: return std::make_unique (size); case Kind::Square: return std::make_unique (size); } return nullptr; } auto s = makeShape(Kind::Circle, 2.0); // caller sees only Shape std::cout area(); Return unique_ptr : clear ownership, no leaks, convertible to shared_ptr . The base needs a virtual destructor . A new type means editing the factory. Avoid with a registry : map<string, function<unique_ptr<Shape>(double)>> (Open/Closed). Factory Method : subclasses decide what to create. Abstract Factory : families of related objects (a matching Button and Checkbox per platform)."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s4","h":"Strategy","x":"Strategy Checkout holds a strategy PricingStrategy apply(price) Regular: price Member: price * 0.9 a lambda The client picks the algorithm; modern C++ often uses a lambda instead of a class. Intent: interchangeable algorithms chosen at runtime without changing the user (sort order, pricing, retry policy). // 1. Classic: an interface and several implementations struct PricingStrategy { virtual double apply(double price) const = 0; virtual ~PricingStrategy() = default; }; struct Regular : PricingStrategy { double apply(double p) const override { return p; } }; struct Member : PricingStrategy { double apply(double p) const override { return p * 0.90; } }; class Checkout { std::unique_ptr strategy_; public: explicit Checkout(std::unique_ptr s) : strategy_(std::move(s)) {} double total(double price) const { return strategy_->apply(price); } }; // 2. Modern: the strategy IS a callable. No class hierarchy needed. class Checkout2 { std::function pricing_; public: explicit Checkout2(std::function f) : pricing_(std::move(f)) {} double total(double price) const { return pricing_(price); } }; Checkout2 c([](double p) { return p * 0.70; }); // a lambda is a strategy // 3. Compile-time: the strategy is a template parameter (policy-based design): zero overhead template class Checkout3 { /* ... Pricing{}(price) ... */ }; Try it: Strategy vs State Similar shape, different intent. Strategy : the client picks the algorithm; strategies are independent. State : the object changes its own behaviour as its state changes, and states trigger the transitions."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s5","h":"Observer","x":"Observer Subject set(value) update(v) Display Logger Alarm the subject does not know who they are Watch for dangling observers, and iterate over a copy while notifying. Intent: a subject notifies its observers on change without knowing who they are (events, signals, pub/sub). Try it: class Observer { public: virtual void update(double value) = 0; virtual ~Observer() = default; }; class Subject { std::vector observers_; public: void attach(Observer* o) { observers_.push_back(o); } void detach(Observer* o) { observers_.erase(std::remove(observers_.begin(), observers_.end(), o), observers_.end()); } void set(double v) { value_ = v; auto copy = observers_; // iterate a COPY: an observer may detach itself in update() for (auto* o : copy) o->update(v); } private: double value_ = 0; }; // Modern, lighter version: observers are just callbacks std::vector > listeners; listeners.push_back([](double t) { std::cout Classic Observer bugs Dangling observer: subject calls a destroyed object. Detach in the destructor (RAII) or store weak_ptr . Modifying the list while notifying: iterate a copy. Threads: lock, but never while calling observers (deadlock risk). Step through a notification:"},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s6","h":"State","x":"State Intent: behaviour changes with internal state, replacing scattered if (state == ...) checks (a turnstile). Send events: // 1. Simplest: an enum and a switch. Fine for a few states. enum class State { Locked, Unlocked }; class Turnstile { State s_ = State::Locked; public: void coin() { s_ = State::Unlocked; } void push() { if (s_ == State::Unlocked) s_ = State::Locked; else alarm(); } }; // 2. Classic State pattern: one class per state struct TurnstileState { virtual std::unique_ptr coin() = 0; // each state returns the NEXT state virtual std::unique_ptr push() = 0; virtual ~TurnstileState() = default; }; // 3. Modern: std::variant of state types + std::visit; the compiler checks every state is handled. using StateV = std::variant ; Use it when valid operations depend on \"what mode am I in\": connections, parsers, protocol handlers. Explicit transitions make illegal states unrepresentable and testable."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s7","h":"Other patterns worth recognising","x":"Other patterns worth recognising Pattern Idea Where you already see it in C++ RAII object owns a resource and releases it in its destructor vector , unique_ptr , lock_guard Adapter wrap an interface so it matches the one clients expect std::stack (adapts deque ), wrapping a C library Decorator wrap an object to add behaviour while keeping its interface buffered/compressed stream wrappers, logging wrappers Proxy a stand-in that controls access to another object shared_ptr , vector<bool>::reference Command turn a request into an object (queue it, undo it) task queues of std::function , thread pools Iterator traverse a collection without exposing its structure the entire STL Visitor add operations to a type hierarchy without changing it std::visit with std::variant Builder construct a complex object step by step fluent config objects: Req().url(u).timeout(5).build() PIMPL hide implementation behind a pointer to keep headers stable and compile times low library public headers CRTP a class derives from a template instantiated with itself: static polymorphism mix-ins, std::enable_shared_from_this // CRTP: compile-time polymorphism, no vtable template struct Shape { double area() const { return static_cast (this)->areaImpl(); } }; struct Circle : Shape { double r = 1; double areaImpl() const { return 3.14159 * r * r; } };"},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s8","h":"SOLID principles","x":"SOLID principles Patterns are tools; SOLID says why they work. Principle In one sentence Pattern that serves it S Single Responsibility a class has one reason to change split parsing, logic and I/O into separate classes O Open/Closed open for extension, closed for modification: add behaviour by adding code, not editing old code Strategy, Factory registry, Decorator L Liskov Substitution a derived object must be usable wherever the base is expected without breaking correctness correct inheritance hierarchies I Interface Segregation prefer several small interfaces over one fat one narrow abstract classes D Dependency Inversion depend on abstractions, not concrete classes; inject dependencies Strategy, Observer, dependency injection (instead of Singleton) Notes: Module 4 , examples ."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s9","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet How do you make a Singleton thread-safe in C++? Use a function-local static (Meyers Singleton). Since C++11 its initialization is guaranteed thread-safe and happens exactly once. Alternatives are std::call_once with an std::once_flag , or creating it before threads start. Hand-written double-checked locking is easy to get wrong. Remember the object's own methods still need locking if they mutate shared state. What is wrong with Singleton? It is global mutable state with a nice name: hidden dependencies, tight coupling, hard to test or mock, awkward lifetime and destruction order, and a problem for multithreading. Prefer passing a reference (dependency injection) unless a single shared instance is truly inherent. Strategy vs State? Both delegate behaviour to a swappable object. Strategy: the client selects an interchangeable algorithm and strategies are independent. State: the context changes its behaviour as its own state changes, and transitions are driven by the states or context. What problems can Observer cause? Dangling observers (destroyed but still registered), modification of the observer list during notification, unexpected notification order, cascading updates and performance issues, and thread-safety issues. Fixes: RAII detach, weak_ptr , iterate over a copy, and do not hold locks while calling out. Why return unique_ptr from a factory? It expresses that the caller takes sole ownership, prevents leaks, and the caller can convert it to a shared_ptr when sharing is needed. Returning a raw owning pointer is ambiguous and error-prone. How do you replace a virtual-function Strategy in modern C++? With a callable: store a std::function (runtime flexibility) or take a template parameter (zero overhead, compile-time choice). A lambda becomes the strategy, and no class hierarchy is needed. What is the Open/Closed principle? Software entities should be open to extension but closed to modification: you add new behaviour (a new strategy, a new shape type registered in a factory) without editing the existing, tested code."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s1","h":"Why threads? The mental model","x":"Why threads? The mental model One process (private memory, isolated from other processes) Thread A own stack + registers Thread B own stack + registers Heap + globals SHARED by every thread this is where races happen Threads share memory; that sharing is the source of both the speed and the bugs. A thread is an independent flow of execution. Several threads make progress at once, like several cooks in one kitchen. Term Meaning Concurrency tasks in progress at overlapping times (can be interleaved on one core) Parallelism tasks running at the same instant on different cores Why: speed (use all cores), responsiveness (keep a UI or network handler alive), hiding I/O waits. Cost: about 1 MB stack each, startup time, context switches. Use threads only where the gain is measurable. The whole difficulty Threads share memory, and the OS can switch between them between any two instructions . std::thread::hardware_concurrency(); // how many hardware threads (cores x hyperthreads) this machine has, e.g. 8"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s2","h":"Your first thread","x":"Your first thread #include #include void hello(int id) { std::cout The order is not fixed . Run it repeatedly and you may see either: main continues worker 1 running worker 1 running or main continues A test that passed once proves almost nothing. Rules Always join() or detach() before the std::thread is destroyed , otherwise std::terminate . A detached thread must not touch anything that may be destroyed. Arguments are copied into the thread. Use std::ref to pass a reference. Threads are move-only : std::thread b = std::move(a); . An exception escaping the thread function calls std::terminate . Catch inside, or pass it out with a future . void addOne(int& n) { ++n; } int x = 0; std::thread t1(addOne, std::ref(x)); // std::ref: pass x by reference (without it this will not even compile) t1.join(); // x is now 1 std::thread t2([] { std::cout Classic detach bug void f() { int local = 0; std::thread t([&] { ++local; }); // captures local BY REFERENCE t.detach(); } // f returns, local is destroyed, the thread still writes to it Capture by value (or join() ) if the thread can outlive the variable. Converted too late void f(int i, const std::string& s); void oops() { char buffer[1024]; std::thread t(f, 3, buffer); // copies the char* POINTER, not the text t.detach(); // oops() may return before the thread builds the std::string } // fix: std::thread t(f, 3, std::string(buffer)); The conversion to std::string happens later, inside the new thread. Convert on the calling side. Join on every path If anything before join() can throw, the thread stays joinable and the program terminates. Use RAII: class ThreadGuard { std::thread& t_; public: explicit ThreadGuard(std::thread& t) : t_(t) {} ~ThreadGuard() { if (t_.joinable()) t_.join(); } // joins however the scope is left ThreadGuard(const ThreadGuard&) = delete; ThreadGuard& operator=(const ThreadGuard&) = delete; }; void f() { std::thread t(work); ThreadGuard g(t); // joins when f exits, even by an exception mayThrow(); } Why no implicit join or detach? A join hides long waits; a detach leaves a thread using dead locals. If a class owns a std::thread , declare it last among the members. C++20: std::jthread Joins automatically in its destructor and supports std::stop_token cancellation."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s3","h":"Shared data and data races","x":"Shared data and data races time Thread 1 read: 0 0 + 1 write 1 Thread 2 read: 0 0 + 1 write 1 counter 0 1 still 1 Expected 2, got 1: both threads read 0 before either wrote. One update is lost. A data race: the two read-modify-write sequences overlap. ++counter is three steps, not one. Two threads each increment a shared counter: int counter = 0; void work() { for (int i = 0; i ++counter is three CPU steps: load the value from memory into a register: r = counter add one to the register: r = r + 1 store the register back to memory: counter = r If B loads between A's load and store, one increment is lost . Play the scheduler with Step A and Step B : Hint: A loads, B runs fully, A finishes. Or press Run 500 random schedules . Caveat The simulator switches far more often than a real OS. A real race may fail once in thousands of runs, which is why it survives testing. Data race = undefined behavior Two threads access the same memory, at least one writes, no synchronization. A race condition is the wider timing-dependent logic bug. Why it hurts: shared data has an invariant (links agree, count matches items). An update breaks it briefly, and a thread looking then sees corruption. Trouble starts when two or more pieces of data change together. Three ways to be safe Strategy How 1. Do not share own data per thread (locals, thread_local ), merge afterwards. Best option. 2. Share, never modify const data is safe to read from many threads. 3. Share and protect a mutex or an atomic ."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s4","h":"Mutexes: taking turns","x":"Mutexes: taking turns time Thread 1 lock, critical section, unlock Thread 2 blocked, waiting lock, critical section, unlock Only one thread is inside the protected region at any moment, so the updates cannot overlap. Cost: thread 2 does nothing while it waits. Keep critical sections short. A mutex lets one thread at a time into the critical section ; others wait at lock() . Same two threads, now locked: Run 500 random schedules: always 4 . std::mutex m; int counter = 0; void work() { for (int i = 0; i lock(m); // locks now, unlocks automatically at the end of the block ++counter; } } Use RAII locks, never raw lock()/unlock() An exception or early return between lock() and unlock() leaves the mutex locked forever. Type Use it when std::lock_guard<std::mutex> default: lock for the whole scope std::unique_lock<std::mutex> unlock early, relock, defer; required by cv.wait std::scoped_lock (C++17) several mutexes at once, deadlock-free std::shared_mutex + shared_lock many readers or one writer Protecting a class class SafeCounter { mutable std::mutex m_; // mutable: so const getters can still lock it int value_ = 0; public: void increment() { std::lock_guard lk(m_); ++value_; } int get() const { std::lock_guard lk(m_); return value_; } }; Good locking Keep critical sections short ; do slow work (I/O, computation) outside. Mutex next to its data ; never hand out references to that data. Never call unknown code (callbacks, virtuals) while locked. Too much locking means contention : the program becomes serial."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"interface-races","h":"Races hidden in interfaces","x":"Races hidden in interfaces A mutex in every member function is necessary but not sufficient . Two threads run this on a shared stack whose methods all lock internally: SafeStack s; // shared by several threads; push, pop, top, empty all lock internally if (!s.empty()) { // 1. is there anything to take? int v = s.top(); // 2. read the top element s.pop(); // 3. remove it use(v); } Each call is atomic, the sequence is not . Play A and B on a one-element stack: Two failures, neither preventable by internal locking: B calls top() on an empty stack: undefined behavior (the empty() answer went stale). Both read top() before either pops: one element processed twice . This check-then-act race also hits lock-free structures. The cause is the interface, so change the interface. Fix: offer whole operations template class SafeStack { std::stack s_; mutable std::mutex m_; public: void push(T v) { std::lock_guard lk(m_); s_.push(std::move(v)); } std::optional try_pop() { // check + read + remove as ONE atomic operation std::lock_guard lk(m_); if (s_.empty()) return std::nullopt; T v = std::move(s_.top()); s_.pop(); return v; } // empty() may still exist, but treat its answer as a hint only: it can be stale the moment it returns. }; if (auto v = s.try_pop()) use(*v); // no window between the check and the removal Why does std::stack split top() and pop()? If pop() returned by value and the copy threw after removal, the element would be lost. A concurrent version must merge them: out-parameter, std::optional , shared_ptr<T> , or a non-throwing move. Same pattern elsewhere: Racy sequence Whole-operation fix if (!m.contains(k)) m[k] = v; one insert / try_emplace call that does both under the lock if (q.size() < max) q.push(x); a try_push that checks and pushes under one lock if (!file_exists(p)) create(p); an atomic \"create if absent\" call (for example the OS O_EXCL flag) if (counter.load() > 0) counter--; a compare_exchange loop Interview angle \"Mutex in every method, so thread-safe?\" No: each call is safe, the caller's sequence is not. Provide combined operations like try_pop and protect the whole invariant ."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s5","h":"Deadlock","x":"Deadlock A deadlock : threads wait for each other forever. A holds a wants b ; B holds b wants a . Step A once, B once, then try to go on: \"Run 500 random schedules\": many end in deadlock. In real programs it may occur once in a million runs, only in production. It needs all four (Coffman conditions); remove one and it cannot happen: Mutual exclusion : one holder at a time Hold and wait : holds one lock, waits for another No preemption : a lock cannot be taken away Circular wait : A waits for B, B for A Fix: one global lock order If every thread takes a before b , no cycle is possible: std::mutex a, b; void thread1() { std::scoped_lock lk(a, b); /* ... */ } // C++17: locks both with a deadlock-avoidance algorithm void thread2() { std::scoped_lock lk(b, a); /* ... */ } // OK even though the order differs: scoped_lock sorts it out // pre-C++17: std::lock(a, b); // lock both atomically (deadlock-free) std::lock_guard la(a, std::adopt_lock); // adopt the already-held locks so RAII still unlocks std::lock_guard lb(b, std::adopt_lock); Four guidelines, one idea Do not wait for a thread that might be waiting for you. Avoid nested locks. If you need several, take them in one step with std::scoped_lock . No user-supplied code under a lock. A callback may take another lock, or keep a pointer to your data: class Wrapper { Data data_; std::mutex m_; public: template void process(F f) { std::lock_guard lk(m_); f(data_); // hands a reference to the protected data to unknown code } }; Data* leaked; wrapper.process([](Data& d) { leaked = }); // the callback keeps the pointer leaked->modify(); // later: modifies the data with NO lock held Never pass out a pointer or reference to protected data. Fixed lock order. Hand-over-hand locking on list nodes deadlocks when two threads walk in opposite directions. Fix the traversal direction. Lock hierarchy. Each mutex has a level; a thread may only lock a lower level than it holds. Violations throw the first time, even if the unlucky timing never occurs. class HierarchicalMutex { std::mutex m_; const unsigned level_; unsigned previous_ = 0; static thread_local unsigned current_; // lowest level this thread currently holds public: explicit HierarchicalMutex(unsigned level) : level_(level) {} void lock() { if (current_ a(high); std::lock_guard b(low); } // OK: 10000 then 5000 { std::lock_guard a(low); std::lock_guard b(high); } // throws at once No mutex needed Two threads that join() each other deadlock, as does any cycle of futures or condition-variable waits. std::thread tA, tB; // both threads are started elsewhere, running funcA and funcB void funcA() { tB.join(); } // A waits for B to finish... void funcB() { tA.join(); } // ...and B waits for A to finish: neither ever returns Interview angle \"Prevent deadlock?\" Global lock order; std::scoped_lock ; no foreign code under a lock; short locks; no nesting; try_lock with back-off. Re-locking a non-recursive mutex you own is undefined behavior."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s6","h":"Atomics","x":"Atomics std::atomic<T> makes operations on one variable indivisible, usually without blocking. The increment is now one step: #include std::atomic counter{0}; counter++; // one indivisible read-modify-write counter.fetch_add(1); // same thing, explicit int v = counter.load(); // atomic read counter.store(5); // atomic write std::atomic stop{false}; // a very common use: a shutdown flag void worker() { while (!stop.load()) { /* work */ } } // main: stop.store(true); t.join(); // compare_exchange: \"set it to desired ONLY if it still equals expected\" int expected = counter.load(); while (!counter.compare_exchange_weak(expected, expected * 2)) { // someone changed it: `expected` was refreshed to the current value, so just retry } Use an atomic for Do not use it for a counter, flag, swapped pointer, statistics an invariant over several variables: use a mutex Memory ordering Compilers and CPUs reorder memory operations; a plain int is not guaranteed visible to other threads. Atomics take a memory order: Order Meaning Use seq_cst (default) one global order for all threads when in doubt release / acquire a release store publishes all prior writes to a thread whose acquire load sees it publishing data with a flag relaxed atomicity only counters, statistics int data = 0; std::atomic ready{false}; // producer thread // consumer thread data = 42; while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) { } ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release); std::cout volatile is NOT thread-safe It only stops the compiler optimising an access away. No atomicity, no ordering. Use std::atomic . Interview angle Atomic vs mutex; is_lock_free() ; seq_cst is the safe default; the ABA problem (CAS succeeds after A to B to A; fix with version counters or hazard pointers)."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s7","h":"Condition variables","x":"Condition variables Producer lock, push, notify shared queue + mutex Consumer wait(lock, pred) push pop notify_one() wakes the sleeping consumer The consumer sleeps (no CPU) until notified, then re-checks the predicate to survive spurious wakeups. Condition variable: sleep until something changed, always paired with a mutex and a condition. To wait until something happens , a busy loop burns 100% CPU. A condition variable lets the thread sleep until woken, always together with a mutex and the shared state. std::mutex m; std::condition_variable cv; std::queue q; // CONSUMER std::unique_lock lk(m); cv.wait(lk, [] { return !q.empty(); }); // sleep until the predicate is true int x = q.front(); q.pop(); // PRODUCER { std::lock_guard lk(m); q.push(42); } cv.notify_one(); // wake one waiting thread Watch the hand-over: Three rules interviewers check Always wait with a predicate. Spurious wakeups happen, and another consumer may take the item first. Change the state under the mutex , then notify. Otherwise a lost wakeup: the waiter sees false, you notify, it then sleeps forever. The waiter needs unique_lock : wait unlocks while sleeping. notify_one() wakes one waiter; notify_all() wakes all (shutdown, or several may proceed). A reusable thread-safe queue template class SafeQueue { std::queue q_; std::mutex m_; std::condition_variable cv_; public: void push(T v) { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); q_.push(std::move(v)); } cv_.notify_one(); // notify AFTER releasing the lock: the woken thread can take it at once } T pop() { // blocks until an item exists std::unique_lock lk(m_); cv_.wait(lk, [this] { return !q_.empty(); }); T v = std::move(q_.front()); q_.pop(); return v; } };"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s8","h":"Futures and async","x":"Futures and async To run a function in the background and get its result later , use a std::future<T> . #include int slowSquare(int n) { std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1)); return n * n; } std::future f = std::async(std::launch::async, slowSquare, 7); // starts on another thread doOtherWork(); // main keeps working meanwhile int result = f.get(); // wait if needed, then get 49 f.get() blocks until ready (once only); wait() and wait_for() do not take the value. Exceptions travel : a throwing task rethrows from f.get() . Prefer tasks to raw threads : std::thread has no result channel and terminates on exceptions. Pass std::launch::async ; the default may defer the call to get() . An async future blocks in its destructor , so a discarded one makes the call effectively synchronous. // std::promise / std::future: a one-shot channel between two threads you manage yourself std::promise p; std::future f = p.get_future(); std::thread t([&p] { p.set_value(42); }); // the worker fulfils the promise (or p.set_exception(...)) std::cout std::packaged_task routes a function's result into a future (how pools return results). Step through a promise and its future:"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s9","h":"Thread pools and producer/consumer","x":"Thread pools and producer/consumer Tasks task task task Task queue mutex + cv Worker 1 Worker 2 Worker N fixed number, created once Reusing N threads avoids the cost of one thread per task and keeps the thread count bounded. Thread pool: many tasks, few long-lived workers. A thread per task is expensive and thousands overload the scheduler. A thread pool starts N workers once; tasks go in a queue ( producer/consumer ). class ThreadPool { std::vector workers_; std::queue > tasks_; std::mutex m_; std::condition_variable cv_; bool stop_ = false; public: explicit ThreadPool(std::size_t n) { for (std::size_t i = 0; i task; { std::unique_lock lk(m_); cv_.wait(lk, [this] { return stop_ || !tasks_.empty(); }); if (stop_ && tasks_.empty()) return; // finish remaining tasks, then exit task = std::move(tasks_.front()); tasks_.pop(); } // lock released BEFORE running the task task(); } }); } void submit(std::function f) { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); tasks_.push(std::move(f)); } cv_.notify_one(); } ~ThreadPool() { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); stop_ = true; } // request shutdown under the lock cv_.notify_all(); // wake every sleeping worker for (auto& w : workers_) w.join(); // wait for them to finish } }; ThreadPool pool(std::thread::hardware_concurrency()); pool.submit([] { std::cout It combines everything: mutex, condition variable, stop flag, RAII shutdown. The task runs outside the lock, or only one could run at a time. Sizing: CPU-bound, about hardware_concurrency() workers (more is oversubscription ); I/O-bound, more. Step through the pool:"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s10","h":"Performance and real-time concerns","x":"Performance and real-time concerns Core 0 writes a Core 1 writes b a b ONE 64-byte cache line each write invalidates the other core's copy: the line ping-pongs (false sharing) Fix: alignas(64) so a and b sit on different lines. The variables are independent, but the hardware moves whole cache lines. Correct first, then fast and predictable (feed handlers). Problem What happens Remedy Lock contention threads queue on one mutex, so the program is effectively serial (Amdahl) shorter locks, shard data, per-thread data merged at the end False sharing different variables on one cache line (64 bytes) ping-pong between cores alignas(64) std::atomic<int> c; Allocation on the hot path malloc may lock; latency unpredictable reserve , pools, ring buffers Priority inversion low-priority thread holds a lock a high-priority one needs; a medium one starves the low one priority inheritance, shorter locks Context switches blocking and waking cost microseconds brief spinning, pin threads to cores Lock-free: SPSC ring buffer With one producer and one consumer, two atomic indices need no lock : each is written by one thread, release/acquire publishes the data. A staple of low-latency code. template class SpscQueue { T buf_[N]; std::atomic head_{0}; // next slot to read: written ONLY by the consumer std::atomic tail_{0}; // next slot to write: written ONLY by the producer public: bool push(const T& v) { // producer thread only auto t = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); auto next = (t + 1) % N; if (next == head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // full buf_[t] = v; // write the data... tail_.store(next, std::memory_order_release); // ...then publish it return true; } bool pop(T& out) { // consumer thread only auto h = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); if (h == tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // empty out = buf_[h]; head_.store((h + 1) % N, std::memory_order_release); // free the slot return true; } }; Be honest in interviews Start with a mutex queue, measure, go lock-free only if the lock is the bottleneck. SPSC is the one case simple enough to write confidently."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s11","h":"Debugging and common bugs","x":"Debugging and common bugs Timing bugs vanish under a debugger or a printf (\"Heisenbug\"). Use tools: ThreadSanitizer : -fsanitize=thread -g finds data races and prints both accesses. Run tests with it. Helgrind / DRD (Valgrind): races and lock-order violations. Thread ids in logs; gdb thread apply all bt on a hang. Symptom Likely cause Wrong totals, rare crashes data race Hang, CPU idle deadlock or lost wakeup Hang, CPU 100% busy-wait or livelock Crash at exit detached thread using destroyed data; joinable thread destroyed Slower with more threads lock contention, false sharing, oversubscription Garbled output several threads on std::cout : build the line, print once, or lock Vocabulary Thread-safe : usable from several threads without external locking ( shared_ptr 's reference count, not its pointee). thread_local : one copy per thread. Function-local static init is thread-safe since C++11 (Meyers Singleton). std::call_once(flag, fn) : initialize exactly once. Further reading: your notes in Module 3: multithreading and synchronization (with runnable examples ), the modern-cpp-tutorial concurrency chapter (threads, mutexes, futures, condition variables, atomics and the memory model), and the modern-cpp-features notes on std::thread , std::async , std::scoped_lock and parallel algorithms."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s12","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Say each answer aloud, then open it. What is the difference between a process and a thread? A process has its own address space; threads of one process share memory but each has its own stack and registers. Thread creation and switching are cheaper, communication is easy (shared memory) but needs synchronization. What is a data race? Is it the same as a race condition? A data race is unsynchronised concurrent access to the same memory with at least one write: undefined behavior in C++. A race condition is the wider class of bugs where correctness depends on timing, which can exist even without a data race (two correctly locked steps that needed to be one atomic operation). What happens if a std::thread is destroyed while still joinable, and why? std::terminate is called. The alternatives are worse: an implicit join can hide long waits, and an implicit detach leaves a running thread using locals that may be gone (undefined behavior). So make the thread unjoinable on every path: join in an RAII guard, or use std::jthread (C++20), which joins automatically. lock_guard vs unique_lock vs scoped_lock ? lock_guard : simplest RAII lock for a scope. unique_lock : flexible (unlock/relock, deferred, movable) and needed by condition variables. scoped_lock (C++17): locks multiple mutexes at once with deadlock avoidance. What is a deadlock and how do you prevent it? Threads wait for each other's locks forever. Needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Prevent with a consistent global lock order, std::scoped_lock / std::lock for multiple mutexes, short critical sections, no calls to foreign code under a lock, or try_lock . Why does condition_variable::wait need a predicate / a loop? Spurious wakeups (a thread may wake without a notification) and because the state may change again between the notification and the thread reacquiring the lock. The predicate re-checks the real condition. Why must the shared condition be modified under the mutex? Otherwise the waiter could check the condition (false), then the notifier changes it and notifies, then the waiter goes to sleep: a lost wakeup. The mutex makes check-and-sleep atomic with respect to the update. std::atomic vs mutex? An atomic makes operations on a single variable indivisible, usually lock-free and cheap. A mutex protects any critical section, including invariants over several variables, but may block. Use atomics for counters and flags, mutexes for compound state. Is volatile a substitute for atomic ? No. volatile prevents some compiler optimisations but gives no atomicity and no inter-thread ordering. Using it for synchronization is a data race. What are the memory orders? seq_cst (default, strongest, one global order), acquire / release (pair a release store with an acquire load to publish data), relaxed (atomicity only). Start with the default; weaken only with a reason and a proof. std::async vs std::thread ? async returns a future carrying the result and any exception, and may use a pool or defer execution (use launch::async to force a thread). thread gives raw control with no result channel. A future from async blocks in its destructor. What is false sharing? Threads writing different variables on the same cache line force the line to bounce between cores, slowing everything. Fix by padding or aligning per-thread data to the cache line size ( alignas(64) ). Why avoid locks and heap allocation on a real-time hot path? Both can block or take unpredictable time (contention, priority inversion, allocator locks). Use preallocated buffers and pools, per-thread data and lock-free structures such as an SPSC ring buffer to keep latency bounded. Is a function-local static initialised thread-safely? Yes, since C++11 initialization happens exactly once even with concurrent callers (\"magic statics\"). That is why the Meyers Singleton is thread-safe. Using the object afterwards still needs its own synchronization. My class locks a mutex in every method. Is it thread-safe? Not necessarily. Each call is safe on its own, but a caller's sequence of calls is not atomic. The classic case is a shared stack: if (!s.empty()) { v = s.top(); s.pop(); } races, because another thread can pop between the check and the use. The cause is the interface (it is also true of a lock-free stack), so the fix is to provide whole operations such as std::optional<T> try_pop() that check and remove under one lock. Can threads deadlock without any mutex? What are the practical rules against deadlock? Yes: two threads that join() each other, or any cycle of waits on futures or condition variables. The rules all mean \"do not wait for a thread that might be waiting for you\": avoid nested locks (or take several with scoped_lock ), never call user-supplied code or leak references to protected data while holding a lock, lock in a fixed order, and use a lock hierarchy so a wrong order is caught immediately at runtime. How would you find a data race in a large program? Build and run the tests with ThreadSanitizer ( -fsanitize=thread ), and Helgrind for lock-order problems; review shared state and make sure every access has a lock or is atomic; reduce sharing by design."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s1","h":"std::string and std::string_view","x":"std::string and std::string_view std::string s = \"hello world\" (owns the characters) h e l l o w o r l d string_view word { ptr = &s[6], len = 5 } two words, no copy The view is valid only while s is alive and unchanged. Not guaranteed null-terminated. View for read-only parameters; string for anything you store. std::string owns its characters. std::string_view (C++17) is a non-owning pointer plus length: two words, no allocation, valid only while the text lives. #include #include void log(std::string_view msg); // accepts std::string, string literal, char array... without copying std::string s = \"hello world\"; log(s); log(\"literal\"); // no temporary std::string is built auto pos = s.find(\"world\"); // 6 if (pos != std::string::npos) { // find returns npos (a huge value) when nothing matches std::string_view word = std::string_view(s).substr(pos); // \"world\", no copy } Classic bug: dangling view std::string_view bad = std::string(\"temporary\"); // the string dies at the semicolon // 'bad' now points at freed memory: undefined behaviour if you read it std::string_view f() { std::string local = \"oops\"; return local; // returns a view of a dead local } A view must not outlive its string and is not null-terminated : do not pass sv.data() to a C API. SSO: short strings (about 15 chars) live inside the object, no heap allocation. string_view for read-only parameters; std::string for what you store. Building big strings in a loop: append or reserve , not chained + temporaries. Interview angle View when you only read (accepts literals without a temporary); const string& when you need null termination."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s2","h":"Regular expressions: the basics","x":"Regular expressions: the basics A regex describes a set of strings . Match vs search is a favourite question: Function Question it answers std::regex_match Does the whole string match the pattern? std::regex_search Does some part of the string match? (first match; captures in std::smatch ) std::regex_replace Replace every match with a replacement string. #include std::regex re(\"ab+c\"); std::regex_match(\"abbbc\", re); // true : the entire string fits std::regex_match(\"xabbbcx\", re); // false : extra characters around it std::regex_search(\"xabbbcx\", re); // true : found inside Syntax Meaning Example . any character a.b matches aab , axb ? * + 0 or 1, 0 or more, 1 or more ab*c matches ac , abbbc {n} {n,m} exactly n, between n and m ab{3}c matches abbbc [abc] [a-z] [^a-z] one of, a range, none of a[bcd]e (x|y) group and alternation gr(e|a)y matches grey , gray ^ $ start and end of the string ^\\d+$ \\d \\w \\s digit, word character, whitespace \\w+@\\w+ Try patterns ( std::regex uses ECMAScript grammar, like this tester): Backslashes Write \"\\\\d+\" or a raw string R\"(\\d+)\" . POSIX classes like [[:alnum:]] work in std::regex but not in the tester."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s3","h":"Captures, replace and iterating matches","x":"Captures, replace and iterating matches Parentheses make capture groups ; std::smatch holds the whole match at 0, groups after. std::string line = \"temp=21.5C humidity=40%\"; std::regex kv(R\"((\\w+)=([\\d.]+))\"); std::smatch m; if (std::regex_search(line, m, kv)) { // m[0] = \"temp=21.5\" m[1] = \"temp\" m[2] = \"21.5\" std::cout \" 21.5, then humidity -> 40 // replace: $1, $2 refer to groups std::string swapped = std::regex_replace(line, kv, \"$2:$1\"); // \"21.5:tempC 40:humidity%\" (only the matched parts change) Practical limits of std::regex Constructing a regex is expensive : build once, not in a loop. std::regex is slow ; on hot paths use find / from_chars (or RE2, CTRE). Nested quantifiers like (a+)+b can backtrack exponentially: no untrusted input. Invalid pattern: std::regex_error . Interview angle regex_match(\"hello world\", regex(\"hello\")) is false: it must cover the entire string. regex_search is true."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s4","h":"Time with std::chrono","x":"Time with std::chrono Clock steady_clock::now() Time point an instant (t1, t0) t1 - t0 Duration count + unit: 250 ms Elapsed time and timeouts: steady_clock (monotonic). Timestamps for people: system_clock (can jump). Durations are type-safe: seconds and milliseconds cannot be confused. Three ideas, in this order: Duration : count plus unit; type-safe. Time point : an instant since a clock's epoch. Clock : the source of time points. Clock Behaviour Use it for system_clock Wall-clock time. Can jump (NTP, manual changes, daylight handling by the OS). Convertible to time_t . Timestamps, dates shown to people steady_clock Monotonic : never goes backwards. Measuring elapsed time, timeouts high_resolution_clock Smallest tick the implementation has. Often just an alias of one of the above. Rarely the right choice; prefer steady_clock #include #include using namespace std::chrono_literals; auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); std::this_thread::sleep_for(250ms); // duration literal auto t1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); auto elapsed = std::chrono::duration_cast (t1 - t0); std::cout (s); // 1 (truncates) steady_clock for intervals system_clock can jump, giving negative or wrong intervals: bad for timeouts."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s5","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. When is a string_view dangerous? When it outlives the string it views: views of temporaries, of locals returned from a function, or of a string that is later modified or reallocated. regex_match versus regex_search ? regex_match must match the whole input. regex_search finds a match anywhere inside it. How do you iterate over every match? std::sregex_iterator (or std::cregex_iterator for C strings) over the range, compared with a default-constructed end iterator. Which clock for a timeout, and why? steady_clock , because it is monotonic and unaffected by changes to the system time. Why can seconds = milliseconds not compile but milliseconds = seconds can? Converting to a coarser unit would truncate, so it requires an explicit duration_cast . Converting to a finer unit is lossless, so it is implicit. Output: what does this print? std::string s = \"abc\"; std::cout << s.find('z') == std::string::npos; It does not compile as intended: << binds tighter than == , so it tries to compare the stream with npos . Parenthesise: std::cout << (s.find('z') == std::string::npos); prints 1."},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s1","h":"Type traits","x":"Type traits A type trait answers a question about a type at compile time ( ::value / _v ) or transforms one ( ::type / _t ). #include static_assert(std::is_integral_v ); // query: true static_assert(!std::is_integral_v ); static_assert(std::is_same_v >); // transform, then compare template T multiply(T a, T b) { static_assert(std::is_arithmetic_v , \"multiply needs a number type\"); return a * b; } // multiply(std::string(\"a\"), std::string(\"b\")); // compile error with YOUR message Explore, especially the references and const rows, where people guess wrong: Interview angle static_assert : compile-time, free. assert : run-time, gone when NDEBUG is defined."},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s2","h":"enable_if and SFINAE","x":"enable_if and SFINAE SFINAE : an invalid substitution drops that overload instead of erroring. std::enable_if uses it to switch overloads by a condition. template , bool> = true> void print(const T& v) { std::cout , bool> = true> void print(const T& v) { std::cout Noisy syntax, poor errors. Better tools: Tool Standard Looks like if constexpr C++17 Branch inside one function; the untaken branch is not even compiled for that type Concepts / requires C++20 template <std::integral T> void print(T v); template void print(const T& v) { if constexpr (std::is_integral_v ) std::cout // C++20: constrained template, readable errors T twice(T v) { return v * 2; } Plain if is not enough With if (is_integral_v<T>) both branches must compile for every T . if constexpr discards the false one."},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s3","h":"constexpr , consteval , constinit","x":"constexpr , consteval , constinit compile time run time constexpr variable always computed constexpr function if arguments are constants otherwise at run time consteval function MUST be compile time error constinit: initialised at compile time, still mutable (avoids static-init-order problems). constexpr does not force compile time; assign to a constexpr variable or use consteval. Write an ordinary function and ask for compile-time evaluation. Keyword Meaning constexpr variable Must be initialised with a constant expression; usable where constants are required (array sizes, template arguments). constexpr function Can run at compile time if its arguments are constants; can still be called at run time with ordinary values. consteval (C++20) An \"immediate\" function: every call must be evaluated at compile time. constinit (C++20) The variable is initialised at compile time (no static-initialisation-order problems), but remains mutable. constexpr int factorial(int n) { return n"},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s4","h":"Template metaprogramming (TMP)","x":"Template metaprogramming (TMP) Before constexpr loops, compile-time computation was recursive template instantiation . You will meet it in old code and interviews. template struct Fact { static constexpr int value = Fact ::value * N; // the compiler instantiates Fact , Fact , ... }; template <> struct Fact { // full specialisation ends the recursion static constexpr int value = 1; }; static_assert(Fact ::value == 120); Each Fact<N> is a separate class; the result is a baked-in constant. Limits: recursion depth cap (a few hundred), slow builds, long errors. Today: constexpr for values, traits + if constexpr /concepts for types; TMP only to compute types . Task Old style Modern style Compute a value recursive struct with ::value constexpr function Pick code by type enable_if overloads if constexpr or concepts Reject bad types obscure substitution errors static_assert / requires"},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s5","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet What does SFINAE stand for and why does it exist? Substitution Failure Is Not An Error: an invalid substitution while deducing a template removes that candidate from overload resolution instead of causing a hard error. It is what makes enable_if overload selection work. std::is_same_v<int, const int> ? False. They are different types. const and references are part of the type, which is why traits like std::decay_t and std::remove_cvref_t exist. constexpr function versus consteval function? constexpr may run at compile time or run time. consteval must run at compile time and is a compile error otherwise. Why is if constexpr better than if in a template? The discarded branch is not instantiated, so code that is invalid for some T does not break the build for that T . How do you avoid writing the factorial struct by hand today? Write constexpr int factorial(int n) and use it in a constexpr context. It is shorter, readable and can also run at run time."},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s1","h":"Why coding rules exist","x":"Why coding rules exist C++ allows code with undefined , unspecified or implementation-defined behaviour. Where a rare fault is unacceptable (cars, medical), projects agree rules that remove the risky corners. Two kinds of rule: Kind Purpose Examples Guidelines (language usage) Avoid errors from using language features in vulnerable or unclear ways initialise every variable; no C-style casts; no unchecked implicit narrowing Style (form) Make code uniform and tidy so reviews are about logic indentation, brace position, naming of classes, functions and members Rules only work if checked automatically ."},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s2","h":"MISRA and AUTOSAR C++14","x":"MISRA and AUTOSAR C++14 MISRA C / C++ : well-known rule sets from automotive, now common in safety-critical embedded. MISRA C++:2008 targets C++03. AUTOSAR C++14 : C++14 guidelines for safety-critical use, based on MISRA C++:2008, updated for smart pointers, move semantics, constexpr. Rules are graded (required, advisory); a justified deviation can be recorded. MISRA C++:2023 supersedes both; check which version the project mandates. Say this in an interview \"I follow the project's coding standard, such as AUTOSAR C++14, check it with a static analyser in CI, and document any deviation with a reason rather than silently working around the tool.\""},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s3","h":"Typical rules, and why","x":"Typical rules, and why Learn the themes , not rule numbers. Name the problem before opening each answer. int count; if (ready) count = compute(); use(count); // what if ready was false? What is wrong? Reading an uninitialised local is undefined behaviour. Rule theme: initialise every object before use ( int count{}; ). double d = 3.9; int i = (int)d; // silently truncates Base* b = (Base*)someObject; // could be reinterpret, static or const cast: you cannot tell What is wrong? A C-style cast hides which conversion happens and can do dangerous ones silently. Rule theme: use the named casts ( static_cast and so on) so the intent is visible and greppable, and avoid reinterpret_cast and const_cast . Widget* w = new Widget; process(w); // if this throws, w leaks delete w; What is wrong? Manual ownership leaks on early returns and exceptions. Rule theme: manage resources with RAII : std::make_unique<Widget>() , containers, no naked new / delete . Many embedded standards go further and forbid dynamic allocation after start-up, to avoid fragmentation and unpredictable timing. uint8_t level = 300; // wraps silently to 44 int total = a * b; // int overflow is undefined behaviour What is wrong? Values change silently or overflow. Rule themes: no implicit narrowing (use brace-initialisation uint8_t level{300}; which the compiler rejects), use fixed-width types, and check arithmetic that can overflow. int sum(const Node* n) { return n ? n->v + sum(n->next) : 0; } What is wrong? Recursion depth depends on the data, so stack usage cannot be bounded and may overflow. Embedded and safety rules commonly forbid recursion and require loops with a provable upper bound. // util.h using namespace std; // injected into every file that includes util.h What is wrong? It pollutes the global namespace of every includer and can silently change overload resolution. Rule theme: never put using namespace in a header . class Buffer { char* data_; public: Buffer(size_t n) : data_(new char[n]) {} ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; } // copy constructor and copy assignment: not declared }; What is wrong? The compiler-generated copy copies the pointer, so two objects delete the same memory (double free). Rule theme: rule of zero / rule of five . Prefer std::vector<char> and write no special members, or define or delete all five."},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s4","h":"Static analysis and tooling","x":"Static analysis and tooling Compiler -Wall -Wextra Static analyser rules, leaks, nulls (CI) Formatter clang-format Tests + sanitisers run-time behaviour Static layers read the source; the last one runs the program. None of them is a proof. Turn warnings on first: they are free. Static analysers read the source without running it: cppcheck , clang-tidy (free); CodeSonar, Polyspace, Helix QAC (commercial, map findings to MISRA/AUTOSAR IDs). Layer What it catches Compiler warnings ( -Wall -Wextra -Werror ) Many beginner mistakes immediately, at no cost. Turn them on first. Static analyser in CI Rule violations, null dereferences, leaks, dead code across the whole code base Formatter ( clang-format ) Style, so nobody argues about braces in reviews Sanitisers and tests What static analysis cannot prove: run-time behaviour ( -fsanitize=address,undefined ) A clean report is not a proof Static analysers give false positives and miss things. They reduce risk; they do not replace design, review and testing."},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s5","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet What is MISRA, and how does AUTOSAR C++14 relate to it? MISRA is a family of coding guidelines for safe use of C and C++ in critical systems. AUTOSAR C++14 is a guideline for C++14 in safety-related environments, based on MISRA C++:2008 and updated for modern C++. Why would a safety standard forbid recursion? Stack depth cannot be bounded statically, so overflow is possible; an iterative version with a bounded loop can be analysed. Why avoid dynamic allocation in embedded real-time code? Allocation time is unpredictable, the heap can fragment, and allocation can fail at run time. Allocate once at start-up or use fixed-size pools. What is the difference between a coding guideline and a code style? Guidelines restrict how language features are used to avoid errors. Style fixes the appearance of the code: indentation, brace placement, naming. You must break a rule. What do you do? Record a justified deviation through the project's process (comment plus tool suppression referencing it), get it reviewed, and keep it as narrow as possible."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s1","h":"From bytes to values","x":"From bytes to values bytes as they arrive (19-byte Trade message) 'T' length seq (4 bytes) price (8 bytes) qty (4 bytes) copy out, fix byte order, check the length Trade { seq, priceTicks, qty } A buffer holds raw bytes; your program wants typed values. A market-data feed arrives as bytes. The job: turn them into values quickly, correctly and without undefined behavior . Never cast the buffer to a struct pointer. Copy fields out instead. Check the length before every read. A truncated or corrupt packet must be rejected, not trusted. Convert byte order explicitly. The network and your CPU may disagree. The tempting wrong way auto* t = reinterpret_cast (buf); // reads fields straight out of the buffer std::cout seq; That one line hides four problems: aliasing (undefined behavior), alignment (the buffer address may not suit a Trade ), padding (the struct layout may not match the wire) and byte order . The next sections take them one by one."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s2","h":"Integers and bit manipulation","x":"Integers and bit manipulation flags = 0x17 = 0001 0111 0 0 0 1 0 1 1 1 bit 7 6 5 4 3 2 1 bit 0 unused venue = 101 = 5 IOC buy Read the venue: (flags >> 2) & 0x7 shift the field down, then mask its width. Feeds pack several fields into one byte; bit operations unpack them. Operation Expression set bit n x | (1u << n) clear bit n x & ~(1u << n) toggle bit n x ^ (1u << n) test bit n (x >> n) & 1u power of two? x != 0 && (x & (x - 1)) == 0 count set bits std::popcount(x) (C++20), or loop x &= x - 1 (clears the lowest set bit each time) constexpr std::uint8_t kBuy = 1u > 2) & 0x7u; } std::uint8_t flags = kBuy | kIoc | (5u Rules that bite Use fixed-width types ( std::uint8_t , std::uint32_t , std::int64_t ) for anything on the wire; int and long change size between platforms ( long is 32-bit on Windows). Unsigned overflow wraps (well defined); signed overflow is undefined behavior . Shifting by the type's width or more is undefined: write 1u << n with n < 32 . Do bit work on unsigned types. Small types are promoted to int in arithmetic. A plain char may be signed, so widening char(0xFF) to int gives -1 : read bytes as unsigned char or std::uint8_t . Predict Promotion and wrap-around std::uint8_t a = 200, b = 100; std::cout 300 44 a + b is computed as an int (both promote), so it is 300 . Storing it back into a uint8_t keeps only the low 8 bits: 300 mod 256 = 44 ."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s3","h":"Struct layout, alignment and padding","x":"Struct layout, alignment and padding struct { char a; int b; char c; } 12 bytes a padding b (4 bytes) c padding offset 0 4 8 12 struct { int b; char a; char c; } 8 bytes b (4 bytes) a c padding Same members, 33% smaller: put the largest members first. The compiler inserts padding so every member sits at a multiple of its alignment. Each type has an alignment ( alignof ): an int must start at a multiple of 4, a double or int64_t at a multiple of 8. The compiler pads between members to guarantee that, and pads the end so that arrays of the struct stay aligned. So sizeof is often bigger than the sum of the members. Step through the layout: struct Careless { char a; int b; char c; }; // 12 bytes struct Ordered { int b; char a; char c; }; // 8 bytes static_assert(sizeof(Careless) == 12 && alignof(Careless) == 4); static_assert(offsetof(Careless, b) == 4); // offsetof gives the byte offset of a member Packing, and why not to rely on it #pragma pack(push, 1) struct Packed { char a; int b; char c; }; // 6 bytes, no padding: matches a wire layout #pragma pack(pop) Packing is not free A packed member may be unaligned : slower on some CPUs, a fault on others, and taking a reference or pointer to it is unsafe. Do not read the wire into a packed struct and use it directly. Read the bytes with memcpy (next sections) and keep ordinary structs inside your program. static_assert(sizeof(...)) is a cheap guard against accidental layout changes."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s4","h":"Endianness","x":"Endianness value 0x12345678 stored at addresses +0 to +3 little-endian (x86, ARM) 78 56 34 12 big-endian = network byte order 12 34 56 78 +0 +1 +2 +3 Little-endian stores the least significant byte first, big-endian the most significant first. Same value, different byte order in memory. Endianness is the order of a multi-byte value's bytes in memory. Network protocols traditionally use big-endian (\"network byte order\"); most desktop and server CPUs are little-endian. Exchanges differ, so read each protocol's specification. Convert at the boundary : when bytes enter the program, turn them into native values, and stay native inside. Tools: ntohl / htonl (sockets), std::endian::native (C++20) to test the CPU, std::byteswap (C++23). The portable way that works everywhere is to assemble the value from bytes with shifts : constexpr std::uint32_t readBe32(const unsigned char* p) { return (std::uint32_t{p[0]} Widen each byte to uint32_t before shifting, otherwise it promotes to a signed int and shifting a large value into the top bit is a trap. The same code works on little- and big-endian machines, and compilers turn it into a single load plus a byte swap."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s5","h":"Reading bytes safely: aliasing and memcpy","x":"Reading bytes safely: aliasing and memcpy Strict aliasing: you may access an object only through a pointer or reference of its own type (or a few related ones). Reading a uint32_t out of memory that holds something else is undefined behavior, and the optimizer is allowed to assume it never happens. Technique Defined? Notes *reinterpret_cast<const uint32_t*>(buf + 1) no aliasing violation, and buf + 1 is probably misaligned std::memcpy(&v, buf + 1, sizeof v) yes compilers turn it into a plain load; no alignment requirement on buf std::bit_cast<To>(from) (C++20) yes same size, trivially copyable types, e.g. float to its bits assemble with shifts ( readBe32 ) yes also fixes byte order write through a union and read another member no in C++ works as a GCC extension, but is not portable C++ view an object as unsigned char , char or std::byte yes the one exception: these may alias anything (but not the reverse) std::uint32_t v; std::memcpy(&v, buf + 1, sizeof v); // native byte order: add a swap if the wire is big-endian std::bit_cast (1.0f); // 0x3f800000: the bits of a float Interview angle \"Why is casting the receive buffer to a struct pointer wrong?\" Aliasing (UB), alignment, padding that may not match the wire, and byte order. \"What do you do instead?\" memcpy or shifts per field, after checking the length."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s6","h":"Zero-copy parsing","x":"Zero-copy parsing A zero-copy parser never copies the message: it keeps views ( std::string_view , std::span ) into the receive buffer and a cursor that advances. No allocation, no copying, no exceptions on the hot path. struct Field { int tag; std::string_view value; }; // \"35=D|49=XPAR|34=12345|\" -> (35,\"D\") (49,\"XPAR\") (34,\"12345\") std::optional nextField(std::string_view& rest) { auto bar = rest.find('|'); if (bar == std::string_view::npos) return std::nullopt; // incomplete: reject std::string_view item = rest.substr(0, bar); rest.remove_prefix(bar + 1); // advance the cursor auto eq = item.find('='); if (eq == std::string_view::npos) return std::nullopt; Field f{}; auto r = std::from_chars(item.data(), item.data() + eq, f.tag); if (r.ec != std::errc{}) return std::nullopt; f.value = item.substr(eq + 1); // a view: no copy return f; } std::from_chars (C++17) converts text to numbers with no allocation, no exceptions and no locale ; it returns an error code and the pointer where it stopped. Compare with stoi (throws, needs a std::string ) and stringstream (slow). Validate everything before trusting it: length fields, tags, digits. Corrupt input is normal; return std::nullopt or an error code instead of throwing. A view is valid only while the buffer is alive and unchanged . If the buffer is reused for the next packet, copy out what you need first. Step through the parse of a binary message (all integers big-endian):"},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s7","h":"Prices: fixed-point versus double","x":"Prices: fixed-point versus double 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3; // false 0.1 + 0.2; // 0.30000000000000004 double cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly, so sums and comparisons drift. For money and prices use an integer count of ticks : 101.2500 with a tick of 0.0001 is 1012500 . class Price { std::int64_t ticks_; // 1 tick = 0.0001 public: static constexpr std::int64_t kScale = 10000; constexpr Price operator+(Price o) const { return Price(ticks_ + o.ticks_); } constexpr auto operator (const Price&) const = default; // exact comparison, no epsilon static std::optional parse(std::string_view s); // \"101.25\" -> 1012500, rejects a 5th decimal }; // 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is true here: 1000 + 2000 == 3000 Exact and deterministic : the same inputs give the same bits on every machine, and equality needs no epsilon. Parse from text directly into ticks; never go through double . Watch overflow : int64_t holds about 9.2e18 ticks (9.2e14 price units at 4 decimals). Multiplying price by quantity can exceed that: check, or use a wider type ( __int128 in GCC and Clang). Decide the rounding rule (down, half-up, half-even) once, and apply it in one place."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s8","h":"Sequence numbers and gaps","x":"Sequence numbers and gaps seq 100 seq 101 102 missing seq 103 expected 102, got 103: GAP in order: expected = got + 1 Each message carries a sequence number; a jump means something was lost. Received versus expected Meaning Typical action equal in order process, expect seq + 1 higher gap : messages were lost buffer new messages, request a retransmission or a snapshot lower duplicate or old ignore enum class SeqResult { InOrder, Duplicate, Gap }; SeqResult classify(std::uint32_t expected, std::uint32_t received) { auto diff = static_cast (received - expected); // unsigned subtraction wraps, then read it as signed if (diff == 0) return SeqResult::InOrder; return diff Wrap-around: a 32-bit counter returns to 0 after 4294967295. Comparing with < breaks at that point; the unsigned subtraction followed by a signed reinterpretation keeps working as long as the two values are within 2 31 of each other. Expecting 4294967295 and receiving 0 is a gap of one , not a duplicate."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Wire Formats and Parsing","id":"s9","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. Why not cast the receive buffer to a struct pointer? Four problems at once: it violates strict aliasing (undefined behavior), the buffer address may not be aligned for the struct, struct padding may not match the wire layout, and the byte order may differ. Check the length, then copy each field out with memcpy or assemble it with shifts. What is struct padding and how do you reduce it? The compiler inserts unused bytes so each member sits at a multiple of its alignment, and pads the end so arrays stay aligned. sizeof can exceed the sum of the members. Order members from largest to smallest alignment; use static_assert(sizeof(...)) to guard the size. What does #pragma pack(1) do, and what does it cost? It removes padding so the struct matches a wire layout. The cost: members may be unaligned, which is slower or faults on some CPUs, and references or pointers to them are unsafe. Prefer reading fields with memcpy . What is endianness? Which does the network use? The order of a multi-byte value's bytes in memory. Little-endian stores the least significant byte first (x86, ARM), big-endian the most significant first. Network byte order is big-endian, though a given exchange protocol may differ, so check the spec. Convert at the boundary. How do you read a big-endian 32-bit value portably? Assemble it from the bytes with shifts, widening each byte to uint32_t first: (uint32_t{p[0]} << 24) | (uint32_t{p[1]} << 16) | (uint32_t{p[2]} << 8) | uint32_t{p[3]} . It works on any CPU. What is strict aliasing? The rule that an object may only be accessed through an lvalue of its own type (or a few related types); the compiler assumes this when optimizing. Exceptions: char , unsigned char and std::byte may alias anything. Use memcpy or std::bit_cast to reinterpret bytes. memcpy , bit_cast or a union for type punning? memcpy and std::bit_cast (C++20) are well defined and compile to a plain load. Reading a different union member than was written is undefined behavior in C++, even though GCC allows it as an extension. How do you set, clear and test a bit, and check for a power of two? Set x | (1u << n) ; clear x & ~(1u << n) ; test (x >> n) & 1 . Power of two: x != 0 && (x & (x - 1)) == 0 . Use unsigned types and keep n below the width. Why should prices not be double ? Binary floating point cannot represent most decimals exactly ( 0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3 ), so sums and comparisons drift. Store an integer count of ticks (a scaled int64_t ): exact, deterministic, and comparable with == . Parse text straight into ticks and watch for overflow when multiplying by quantity. Why std::from_chars in a parser? It converts text to numbers without allocating, throwing or consulting the locale, and reports errors through a return value plus the position where it stopped. stoi throws and needs a std::string ; stringstream is slow. How do you detect a gap in sequence numbers, and handle wrap-around? Compare the received number with the expected one: equal is in order, higher is a gap (lost messages: buffer and request a retransmission or snapshot), lower is a duplicate. For wrap-around compute static_cast<int32_t>(received - expected) and look at its sign."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s1","h":"Where latency comes from","x":"Where latency comes from Socket bytes arrive Reader thread parse the bytes SPSC queue Book builder update the book Strategy consume updates system calls, copies allocation, exceptions locks, false sharing cache misses, map lookups virtual calls, logging Every stage must be fast AND predictable. One occasional 50 microsecond stall anywhere shows up at the end of the pipeline. A hot path is a chain; the slowest rare event in any link is what you feel. Latency is the time for one message to get through; throughput is how many per second. A system can have great throughput and terrible latency. For a feed handler the tail matters: the rare slow case (once in a thousand) is what causes a stale book or a missed trade. Average time hides it. So the goal is not only \"fast\" but predictable : remove anything whose cost can suddenly jump. Source Rough cost (orders of magnitude) Remedy L1 cache hit versus main memory about 1 ns versus about 100 ns contiguous data, small hot structs Heap allocation ( new ) tens of ns, but unbounded in the worst case (locks, page faults) preallocate, pools, arenas Mutex contention, context switch microseconds single-writer queues, pinning System call (read, write, log) hundreds of ns to microseconds batch, keep off the hot path Page fault microseconds pre-touch memory at startup Throwing an exception microseconds error codes, optional The numbers vary a lot by CPU and system; treat them as ratios. The rule that matters: measure first , then remove the biggest source."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s2","h":"Measuring: percentiles and tail latency","x":"Measuring: percentiles and tail latency 99.9 % about 200 ns 0.1 % about 80 µs 400 times slower, but rare mean 281 ns looks healthy. p50 200 ns. p99 219 ns. p99.9 80,004 ns. The average hides the tail. (Numbers from the program in 12-low-latency.) Never judge by the average. Report percentiles : p50 (median), p99, p99.9, and the maximum. p99.9 means 1 in 1000 operations is at least that slow. Collect every sample (do not average on the fly), sort afterwards, read the percentile by rank. std::uint64_t percentile(const std::vector & sorted, double p) { std::size_t rank = static_cast (p / 100.0 * sorted.size()); return sorted[std::min(rank, sorted.size() - 1)]; } auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); // monotonic: never goes backwards doWork(); auto t1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); samples.push_back(duration_cast (t1 - t0).count()); // reserve() the vector first Measuring pitfalls The compiler deletes unused work. If a benchmark's result is never used, the loop may be optimized away. Store results into a volatile sink, or print a checksum. Warm up. The first iterations pay for cold caches and page faults. Discard them, or report them separately. Do not disturb what you measure: no printing or allocation inside the timed region, and the clock call itself costs tens of nanoseconds. Measure in realistic conditions (real message rates, other threads running). A quiet benchmark hides the stalls that matter."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s3","h":"Memory on the hot path","x":"Memory on the hot path new and malloc can take a lock, walk free lists, call into the kernel for more memory and fragment the heap. Usually fast, but occasionally slow and unpredictable . The rule: allocate everything at startup; the hot path never calls new . Preallocate containers: reserve() a vector so it never reallocates (one allocation instead of several as it grows). Object pool: a fixed array of slots plus a free list . Allocation and release are a few instructions; if the pool is exhausted, you decide what to do (drop, count, wait). Arena ( std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource ): allocation is \"bump a pointer\" inside a buffer you provide; everything is released at once. Also: std::array and fixed buffers instead of std::string and std::vector for small data, and no std::function capturing large state (it may allocate). Step through a pool: template class Pool { union Slot { alignas(T) unsigned char storage[sizeof(T)]; std::size_t nextFree; }; std::array slots_; std::size_t freeHead_ = 0; // first free slot; N means none public: Pool() { for (std::size_t i = 0; i T* allocate(Args&&... args) { if (freeHead_ == N) return nullptr; // exhausted: the caller decides Slot& s = slots_[freeHead_]; freeHead_ = s.nextFree; // pop the free list return new (s.storage) T(std::forward (args)...); // placement new: construct in place } void deallocate(T* p) { p->~T(); auto* s = reinterpret_cast (p); s->nextFree = freeHead_; // push the slot back freeHead_ = s - slots_.data(); } }; Placement new constructs an object in memory that already exists, so no allocation happens. The program in 12-low-latency replaces operator new with a counter and shows 0 heap allocations on the pool path, 0 for a pmr::vector in an arena, and 1 for a vector with reserve(100) ."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s4","h":"Cache-friendly data","x":"Cache-friendly data Array of structs: 40-byte records, a loop reads only the bid bid ask qty time name bid ask qty time name bid ask qty time name 1 record per 64-byte cache line: 8 useful bytes out of every 40 loaded. Struct of arrays: the bids sit together bid bid bid bid bid bid bid bid 8 bids per cache line: every loaded byte is used. The CPU loads whole 64-byte cache lines, not single fields. A cache line is 64 bytes. A cache miss to main memory costs about a hundred times a hit, so locality dominates: data used together should sit together. Contiguous beats linked. std::vector and std::array stream through memory; std::list and std::map chase pointers to nodes scattered across the heap (each node is also a separate allocation). AoS versus SoA. Array of structs keeps one record together (good when you use most fields); struct of arrays keeps each field together (good when a loop uses one or two fields). The program shows 40,000 bytes streamed to read all bids with AoS and 8,000 with SoA, with identical results. Hot/cold split: keep fields used on every message together and small; move rarely used fields elsewhere (struct size and padding: Wire Formats ). False sharing Two threads writing different variables on the same cache line make the line bounce between cores. Give each its own line: alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> head_; alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> tail_; ( sizeof grows from 16 to 128 bytes, which is the point). Introduced in Multithreading ."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s5","h":"Order books: choosing a structure","x":"Order books: choosing a structure bid side: quantity at each price, index = price - base 120 0 340 75 500 0 0 index 0 1 2 3 4 best bid (highest non-empty index) Update = one array write. When the best level empties, scan down to the next one. A bounded price window turns the book into a flat array. An order book keeps the resting quantity at each price on the bid side and the ask side, and updates it on every add, modify or cancel. The questions are always the same: update a level, find the best price, cancel by order id . Structure Update Best price Trade-offs std::map<price, qty> O(log n) O(1) ( begin() ) simple and sorted, but a heap node per level (allocation, pointer chasing) sorted std::vector O(n) shift O(1) contiguous; fine when few levels matter (most activity is near the top of the book) array indexed by tick O(1) O(1) tracked, rescan on empty no allocation, cache-friendly; needs a bounded price window and memory proportional to it hash map orderId -> (price, qty) O(1) average n/a for cancel by id ; an open-addressing table avoids per-node allocation class BookSide { static constexpr std::int64_t kLevels = 4096; std::int64_t base_; // price (ticks) of index 0 std::array qty_{}; std::int64_t bestIdx_ = -1; // -1 when empty public: void add(std::int64_t price, std::uint64_t q) { auto i = price - base_; qty_[i] += q; if (i > bestIdx_) bestIdx_ = i; // O(1) } void remove(std::int64_t price, std::uint64_t q) { auto i = price - base_; qty_[i] = q >= qty_[i] ? 0 : qty_[i] - q; if (qty_[i] == 0 && i == bestIdx_) // the best level vanished: scan down while (bestIdx_ >= 0 && qty_[bestIdx_] == 0) --bestIdx_; } }; The program runs 200,000 random adds and removes against both this array book and a std::map , and checks the best bid after every operation: 0 mismatches. Prices are the integer ticks from Wire Formats ; a price outside the window needs a fallback (a re-centred window or an overflow structure)."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s6","h":"Queues between threads","x":"Queues between threads Pipeline stages run on separate threads and hand messages over a queue. With one producer and one consumer (SPSC) no lock is needed: each index is written by exactly one thread ( Multithreading ). template class SpscRing { static_assert((N & (N - 1)) == 0, \"capacity must be a power of two\"); T buf_[N]; alignas(64) std::atomic head_{0}; // consumer writes alignas(64) std::atomic tail_{0}; // producer writes public: bool tryPush(const T& v) { auto t = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); if (t - head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == N) return false; // full buf_[t & (N - 1)] = v; // mask instead of %: the counters just keep counting up tail_.store(t + 1, std::memory_order_release); return true; } bool tryPop(T& out) { auto h = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); if (h == tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // empty out = buf_[h & (N - 1)]; head_.store(h + 1, std::memory_order_release); return true; } }; Power-of-two capacity turns the wrap-around into index & (N - 1) , one instruction, with free-running counters. Padded indices ( alignas(64) ) so the two threads do not fight over one cache line. Always bounded. An unbounded queue turns a slow consumer into growing memory and growing delay. When the queue is full you choose a policy : Policy Behavior Use when block (back-pressure) the producer waits nothing may be lost, and the producer can afford to wait (not for a market-data reader) drop newest tryPush returns false, count it simple; the book will need a recovery drop oldest overwrite the oldest entry only the latest data matters conflate keep only the latest update per instrument slow consumers of quotes The program runs two real threads over a 1024-slot ring with a drop-newest policy: received + dropped = 200000 and the consumer's sum equals the producer's, in 20 of 20 runs."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s7","h":"A predictable hot path","x":"A predictable hot path Besides memory and queues, a few language features add unpredictable cost. Keep them off the hot path: Avoid on the hot path Why Use instead exceptions ( throw ) a throw costs microseconds and its cost varies; unwinding tables add size error codes, std::optional , a small result struct; validate at the boundary virtual calls an indirect call: no inlining, harder for the CPU to predict templates, CRTP, std::variant with std::visit (decide the type at compile time) locks ( std::mutex ) contention means waiting and context switches single-writer data, SPSC queues, atomics logging and I/O system calls, formatting, locks inside the logger push a small record onto a queue; a background thread formats and writes std::function , shared_ptr possible allocation, an indirect call, an atomic reference count templates or function pointers, unique_ptr or plain ownership std::map , std::list , std::string building allocation per node or per string arrays, pools, fixed buffers, string_view enum class ParseError { None, Truncated, BadType, BadLength }; ParseError parseTrade(const unsigned char* buf, std::size_t len, Trade& out) noexcept { if (len Mark functions that cannot throw noexcept ; it lets the compiler skip unwinding code. constexpr lookup tables move work to compile time; [[likely]] / [[unlikely]] (C++20) hint at the common path. These rules are for the hot path . Startup, configuration and error reporting can use exceptions and heap freely."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s8","h":"Threads, cores and busy-polling","x":"Threads, cores and busy-polling Pin each thread to a core (CPU affinity). The OS stops migrating it, so its caches stay warm and its timing steady. Give hot threads their own cores, and do not run more busy threads than cores ( oversubscription causes context switches). Busy-poll instead of blocking. A thread that sleeps on a condition variable pays a wake-up delay (microseconds) when work arrives. A thread that spins checking a flag reacts almost instantly but uses 100% of its core . Latency-critical threads spin; others block. Memory placement: on multi-socket machines keep a thread and its data on the same NUMA node. Pre-touch (page-fault in) buffers at startup so the first use on the hot path does not take a page fault. Isolating cores from the scheduler and tuning interrupts is an operating-system job, usually done by the platform team. // busy-poll: spin on an atomic instead of sleeping (burns the core, minimal wake-up delay) while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) { _mm_pause(); // x86 hint: be nice to the sibling hyperthread } // Linux only: pin the calling thread to core 3 cpu_set_t set; CPU_ZERO(&set); CPU_SET(3, &set); pthread_setaffinity_np(pthread_self(), sizeof(set), &set); Interview angle \"How would you design a market-data feed handler?\" Reader thread pinned to a core, reading the socket and parsing with zero copy into preallocated structures; an SPSC queue to a book-builder thread, which updates array-based books and publishes; no allocation, locks, exceptions or logging on the path; bounded queues with a stated drop or recovery policy; sequence-number gap detection; and p99.9 latency measured under realistic load."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Low-Latency C++","id":"s9","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. Why does tail latency matter more than the average in a feed handler? The rare slow case (p99.9) is what produces a stale book or a missed trade, and the average hides it: a mean of 281 ns can coexist with a p99.9 of 80 microseconds. Measure and report percentiles, and remove sources of unpredictable delay. Why avoid new on the hot path, and what do you do instead? The allocator can take a lock, walk free lists, fragment and call the kernel, so its cost is unpredictable. Allocate at startup: reserve containers, use an object pool or an arena ( std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource ), and use fixed buffers instead of strings and vectors for small data. How does an object pool work? A fixed array of slots, plus a free list threaded through the free slots. Allocation pops the head of the list and constructs the object there with placement new; deallocation destroys it and pushes the slot back. Both are a few instructions with no heap, and when the pool is empty the caller decides what to do. Array of structs versus struct of arrays? AoS keeps one record's fields together, good when you use most fields. SoA keeps each field in its own array, good when a loop uses one or two fields: it streams only the bytes it needs, so each cache line is fully useful. Same results, less memory traffic. What is false sharing and how do you fix it? Two threads writing different variables on the same 64-byte cache line force the line to bounce between cores. Give each variable its own line with alignas(64) or padding, as for the head and tail indices of an SPSC queue. Why is a vector or array usually faster than std::map or std::list ? Contiguous memory is cache-friendly and prefetchable; node-based containers chase pointers across the heap and allocate per node. Big-O hides these constants, especially for small and medium sizes. How would you structure an order book? For a bounded price window, an array of quantities indexed by price tick: O(1) updates, no allocation, with the best price tracked and rescanned when its level empties. A hash map from order id to its price and quantity handles cancels. A std::map is simpler but allocates a node per level. Why is an SPSC ring capacity a power of two? The wrap-around becomes counter & (N - 1) instead of a modulo, one cheap instruction, and the head and tail counters can simply keep counting up while tail - head is the size. What do you do when a queue between threads is full? Never let it grow without bound. Choose a policy: block (back-pressure), drop the newest, drop the oldest, or conflate to the latest per instrument, count what was dropped, and recover from the gap if needed. A market-data reader normally cannot block. Why avoid exceptions, virtual calls, locks and logging on the hot path? Each adds cost that is large or unpredictable: a throw costs microseconds, a virtual call blocks inlining and is an indirect branch, a mutex can wait and context-switch, and logging means system calls and formatting. Use error codes, templates or CRTP, single-writer data, and an asynchronous logger fed by a queue. Busy-polling versus blocking, and why pin threads? Blocking saves CPU but a wake-up costs microseconds; busy-polling reacts almost immediately but burns a whole core, so only latency-critical threads spin. Pinning a thread to a core stops migration, which keeps its caches warm and its timing stable."}];
